#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106651} :activity/id #uuid "eb407cdf-9aea-5548-a574-970269d4ce5b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048184 :topic/id #uuid "86927b03-ba99-5587-9673-31a5bae6ef0b" :topic/math-academy-id 1317} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106651 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107178 :tutorial/content "The shape below consists of $6$ line segments defined by the $6$ points $A$, $B$, $C$, $D$, $E$, and $F.$ These line segments form a loop. \n\n![](images/25/259bfd166a7870bd1a057b28d4b3324f3b98d05222596f1426afa01d6573e3d8.png)\n\nA chain of straight line segments that form a loop is called a **polygon**.\n\nThe line segments are the **sides** of the polygon, and the endpoints of the sides are the **vertices**.\n\nFor this polygon:\n\n- The $6$ vertices are $A$, $B$, $C$, $D$, $E$, and $F.$\n\n- The $6$ sides are $\\overline{AB}$, $\\overline{BC}$, $\\overline{CD}$, $\\overline{DE}$, $\\overline{EF}$, and $\\overline{AF}.$\n\nTo denote a particular polygon, we start at one vertex and loop around the polygon, reading off the vertices in order. So, the polygon above can be denoted $ABCDEF.$\n\nThe singular form of the word *vertices* is **vertex**. So, $A$ is a vertex, $B$ is a vertex, etc." :tutorial/id #uuid "342100ff-c1a7-531c-90a6-e07a80e83fda" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9674 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5fbe01e1-2066-5d27-b53a-a2fa74fc43e6" :step/math-academy-id 188116 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106652}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106652 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107179 :tutorial/content "The name of a polygon is determined by the number of sides it has. \n\nThe polygon below has $6$ sides. A six-sided polygon is called a **hexagon**.\n\n![](images/25/259bfd166a7870bd1a057b28d4b3324f3b98d05222596f1426afa01d6573e3d8.png)\n\nThe names of the polygons with up to ten sides are given below:\n\n| Number of sides | Name |\n| --- | --- |\n| $3$ | Triangle |\n| $4$ | Quadrilateral |\n| $5$ | Pentagon |\n| $6$ | Hexagon |\n| $7$ | Heptagon |\n| $8$ | Octagon |\n| $9$ | Nonagon |\n| $10$ | Decagon |" :tutorial/id #uuid "9aaf4a28-03c1-57ea-adcd-968b00ad8a41" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9675 :tutorial/title "Naming Polygons"} :step/id #uuid "d4d97dfe-283b-5019-b2e4-5d6ab6550b1d" :step/math-academy-id 188117 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106653}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106653 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105754 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105491 :question/id #uuid "3f0d9dbb-448f-5e1e-b06c-4b6a3a674b09" :question/math-academy-id "e-15616"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9fd1d3c-8479-5b35-abbc-9664cea2e1a8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049350}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106192} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442724} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442725} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442727}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Polygon"} :step/id #uuid "5e398f9a-e437-5d98-bf9e-cb85ba65689b" :step/math-academy-id 188118 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106654}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106654 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107182 :tutorial/content "We can categorize polygons in different ways.\n\nA polygon is **simple** if its boundary does not intersect itself. Otherwise, the polygon is **complex**.\n\n![](images/71/71ef7dbf87cb3a097ec153eb4c4e18920c2567e6a239a788f4340a0a5f33ea6f.png)\n\nA polygon is **convex** if it has no angles pointing inwards. Otherwise, if the polygon has angles pointing inwards, we call it **concave**.\n\n![](images/ef/ef7357c0e21760c3e7f159c253c527e35aac869ce16381132a53356dc53455e2.png)\n\nIt might help to remember that a con**cave** polygon looks a bit like the entrance to a **cave** when viewed from the outside. \n\n![](images/11/11e9844e49a5c586ec573e08865eb7d02dd68459e90df90749116a9eb1575430.png)\n\n**Note**: A more technical way to define a convex polygon is to say that all of its *internal angles* are less than $180^\\circ.$ If you're not sure what an internal angle is yet, don't worry! We'll learn more about the internal angles of a polygon in future lessons." :tutorial/id #uuid "b7c27ce6-fd4a-5560-bc80-0e72700ff320" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9678 :tutorial/title "Types of Polygons"} :step/id #uuid "e39203ba-5fa0-58f6-9ad6-4003cf7c5c77" :step/math-academy-id 188121 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106655}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106655 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105755 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105494 :question/id #uuid "d56604cb-e46a-5d52-94c4-dea5e8c0df3b" :question/math-academy-id "e-15623"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d25a77b-7977-50b2-b375-51b8539bc9b9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442722}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Type of a Polygon"} :step/id #uuid "ac8b8e7b-3a84-5a11-ab36-35cd5a021aa5" :step/math-academy-id 188122 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106656}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106656 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107180 :tutorial/content "Consider the pentagon $ABCDE,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/b1/b11b509e881cd813ff6879a0a6fa9559d51ffcbc5da4c150a0eaf0a9ee82a159.png)\n\nWe have the following definitions: \n\n- Two *sides* are **adjacent** (or **consecutive**) if they have a common vertex.\n\nFor example, $\\color[rgb]{0.753,0.494,0.643}\\overline{BC}$ is adjacent to $\\color[rgb]{0.902, 0.624, 0}\\overline{AB}$ *and* ${\\color[rgb]{0.902, 0.624, 0}\\overline{CD}}.$\n\n![](images/76/760a9cbccd117214d4e93392cdae17bf117497501b05f7b2e25c6c8cb5cd43e1.png)\n\n- Two *vertices* are **adjacent** if there is a side joining them. \n\nFor example, the vertex $E$ is adjacent to the vertices $D$ and $A$.\n\n![](images/6d/6d857920609da14fb0b31f762400c5f54e9e428717c2b55fcb17215280bbbfed.png)\n\n- A **diagonal** is a line segment that joins two non-adjacent vertices. \n\nFor example, ${\\color[rgb]{0.902, 0.624, 0}{\\overline{AC}}}, {\\color[rgb]{0.902, 0.624, 0}{\\overline{BD}}},$ and ${\\color[rgb]{0.902, 0.624, 0}{\\overline{BE}}}$ are diagonals of our pentagon.\n\n![](images/ad/adf7afaa66144a33dba7ff3732b01619bc13cac0fae59c466393d4084c748a59.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "b82f1900-5686-5fcc-8354-6c00b102f576" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9676 :tutorial/title "The Parts of a Polygon"} :step/id #uuid "0d4447dd-331d-5e2c-9e2b-470035c668cb" :step/math-academy-id 188125 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106657}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106657 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105756 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105492 :question/id #uuid "e72f5a3b-8085-54dc-a09a-121925ef509c" :question/math-academy-id "e-15617"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61c58110-9813-5588-81d2-5735718589aa" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106387} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442726} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442731}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Adjacent Vertices and Sides"} :step/id #uuid "a35aa384-e796-5e75-adfa-23d8efbf0c06" :step/math-academy-id 188126 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106658}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106658 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105757 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105493 :question/id #uuid "33bf6630-1558-50b9-8823-0bd58d0787af" :question/math-academy-id "e-15618"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ed0dc2a-7c76-5fee-9aa8-aad529cf1952" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442723} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442729}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of a Polygon"} :step/id #uuid "94356407-759e-59ef-b5e4-6f11d01f5cf9" :step/math-academy-id 188129}] :activity/title "Polygons" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105971}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180626} :activity/id #uuid "171b476e-9322-594d-b1d2-c603a90f1b67" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048207 :topic/id #uuid "886ced30-c0cc-548f-90c0-8a84697856ec" :topic/math-academy-id 3680} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180626 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180777 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to solve the following subtraction problem:\n\n$$\n2 - 4.5\n$$\n\nWe can solve this problem using a number line. \n\nTo subtract $4.5$ from $2,$ we break up $4.5$ into its whole number part ($4$) and its decimal part ($0.5$) and proceed in two steps.\n\n- First, we subtract the whole number part. We start at $2$ on the number line and move $4$ places to the left, which brings us to $-2.$\n\n- Then, we subtract the decimal part. We move the remaining $0.5$ places to the left, leaving us at $-2.5.$\n\n![](images/e7/e79d6ec2413d47e2b2b67237194a057d802746d1b9b016dcf118ca91b2bf31df.png)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\n2 - 4.5 = -2.5.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1e60ed87-2f30-5d4d-9d0b-990a5f1b8e1f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8384 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "8566447b-d96f-5adc-9b6e-c089a3f423a4" :step/math-academy-id 75114 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180627}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180627 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179812 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179458 :question/id #uuid "0e9e4dd1-3161-5d3c-afab-5514e1c2d0d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-13383"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44b96053-1ffa-56d3-8129-15ada765b9a7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048549} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048911} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049097}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444436} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444439} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444440}] :knowledge-point/title "Subtracting Positive Decimals From Decimals With Halves"} :step/id #uuid "6c4541f7-07d8-51f6-b705-9bb2d82f1069" :step/math-academy-id 75115 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180628}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180628 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180778 :tutorial/content "We can use the same methods that we learned to subtract whole numbers in more complicated situations.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the following problem:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{3.5}} - {\\color{blue}{9.8}}\n$$\n\nWe're subtracting a *positive* decimal from a smaller *positive* decimal. So the final answer must be negative. \n\nWe start by solving the following easier problem, which is to carry out the subtraction *in the reverse order*:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{9.8}} - {\\color{red}{3.5}} = 6.3\n$$\n\nHowever, since our final answer must be negative, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{3.5}} - {\\color{blue}{9.8}} = -6.3\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b35021fd-095a-5cca-9a9e-24ce11a93ae2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8385 :tutorial/title "Subtracting Positive Decimals From Positive Decimals"} :step/id #uuid "56a03151-c084-5924-9eca-03e11afc4c98" :step/math-academy-id 75118 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180629}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180629 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180779 :tutorial/content "Now suppose we have the following problem:\n\n$$\n-3.2 - 6.4\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're subtracting a *positive* decimal from a *negative* decimal. So the final answer must be negative. \n\nWe start by solving the following easier problem, which is to *add their absolute values*:\n\n$$\n3.2+6.4 = 9.6\n$$\n\nHowever, since our final answer must be negative, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-3.2 - 6.4 = -9.6\n$$\n\n \n\n**Remember**\n\n- When we subtract a *positive* number from a smaller *positive* number, we carry out the subtraction *in the reverse order* and then make the final answer negative.\n\n- When we subtract a *positive* number from a *negative* number, we *add their absolute values* and then make the final answer negative." :tutorial/id #uuid "93e4bd99-076c-5ac9-9c1b-362ae1e66e8f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8386 :tutorial/title "Subtracting Positive Decimals From Negative Decimals"} :step/id #uuid "a503405f-0d74-5b80-9c37-8d32b6f8da23" :step/math-academy-id 75119 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180630}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180630 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179813 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179459 :question/id #uuid "6bd42661-9814-51ce-a359-39bd54aff3e7" :question/math-academy-id "e-13384"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "507d4d46-699c-5b4f-a010-66aec6abef67" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444444} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444447} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493935}] :knowledge-point/title "Subtracting Positive Decimals From Decimals"} :step/id #uuid "96ee8d90-d6bf-59b8-a1c3-bf6554a1e0f9" :step/math-academy-id 75120 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180631}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180631 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179814 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179460 :question/id #uuid "45b45968-91b2-5ed4-86c2-51d31e02b714" :question/math-academy-id "e-13385"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "778ae1c8-c651-56f1-ab58-1817d3fe4bb9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048588}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179938} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179939} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444437} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444446} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493907}] :knowledge-point/title "Subtracting Positive Decimals From Decimals With Unequal Numbers of Decimals"} :step/id #uuid "ef013d16-ba95-5bba-b84c-4f5db1abc151" :step/math-academy-id 75123}] :activity/title "Subtracting a Positive Decimal From a Smaller Decimal" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179836}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493985} :activity/id #uuid "1faba002-0fe8-5c3a-b172-4c31ffabe97e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048207 :topic/id #uuid "886ced30-c0cc-548f-90c0-8a84697856ec" :topic/math-academy-id 3680} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493985 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179939 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f71a683-7a1e-5254-83aa-435b95cefd8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177304} {:answer/id #uuid "ff238f2b-54fe-52a4-bede-85ada20612d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177305} {:answer/id #uuid "678029b8-8094-59ba-9f30-b8af5b59536a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177306} {:answer/id #uuid "67ba88b2-8670-57a3-ab0a-86430ddf9c80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177307} {:answer/id #uuid "7dd28e8b-8de3-50e0-833f-b5f4958b897e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177308}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177308} :answer-field/id #uuid "990be945-bd50-539c-94a1-aafdbc3b95e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177303}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "07a41ea1-448d-54d3-bef2-faec2900cf35" :question/math-academy-id "q-119565" :question/problem "$3-5.8=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $5.8$ is larger than $3,$ the answer will be negative.\n\nWe start by solving an easier problem, which is to carry out the subtraction in the reverse order:\n\n$$\n5.8-3=2.8\n$$\n\nHowever, since our answer must be negative, we need to change the sign of the answer above. \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n3-5.8=-2.8\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "27274a67-7e81-5979-85d8-f958ecda56fb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494070}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493999 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493935 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f51a08b5-544d-56bf-b524-00c4e402e1fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493786}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493786} :answer-field/id #uuid "89d68d1f-65d8-579a-bcd1-d6d181a6133c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8f920e3a-0555-530e-885c-0505096145f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-186102" :question/problem "$-3.6-2.3=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The answer will be negative since $-3.6$ is negative, and we're subtracting $2.3$ from it.\n\nWe start by solving an easier problem, which is to add their absolute values:\n\n$$\n3.6+2.3=5.9\n$$\n\nHowever, since our answer must be negative, we need to change the sign of the answer above. \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-3.6-2.3=-5.9.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "44f0d32e-1ab0-51b5-842a-9748e41b55ab"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494070 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493907 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2862e84e-f462-5032-8dfa-808b3125fdc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493456} {:answer/id #uuid "41078b77-96a0-5f7c-bb94-1be5ec73d85e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493502} {:answer/id #uuid "823212ce-1cfb-5c75-bb92-ea01b8f7d8f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493597} {:answer/id #uuid "b7bebb8b-c4ab-5271-bbaa-eee64c1d8059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493689} {:answer/id #uuid "de59e0c6-68e6-5941-9e8b-b9a2293c2c62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-14.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493741}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493689} :answer-field/id #uuid "256bfffe-3c5a-55f0-9609-ab2ee806e2e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493810}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2d4d7236-1971-52db-b086-a77353f5fe00" :question/math-academy-id "q-128150" :question/problem "$6-8.7=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $8.7$ is larger than $6,$ the answer will be negative.\n\nWe start by solving an easier problem, which is to carry out the subtraction in the reverse order:\n\n$$\n8.7-6=2.7\n$$\n\nHowever, since our answer must be negative, we need to change the sign of the answer above. \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n6-8.7=-2.7\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f559c8d7-8fa9-505d-b2c5-2de1201ff6c8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493999}}] :activity/title "Subtracting a Positive Decimal From a Smaller Decimal" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493368}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074634} :activity/id #uuid "4417fe29-8f51-5120-ac66-9aaeadadb6f3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048508 :topic/id #uuid "a587db09-9578-5d61-b811-99a95c85061d" :topic/math-academy-id 553} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074634 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075034 :tutorial/content "We can find **equivalent ratios** by multiplying or dividing all numbers in a ratio by the same value. \n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the following ratio:\n\n$$\n2:6\n$$\n\nIf we multiply *both* numbers by $5,$ for example, we get the ratio $10:30.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,2 &  & : & \\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,6 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓\\times \\,5 &  &  &  & \\,\\,↓\\times \\,5 &  \\\\ \\,10 &  & : &  & \\,30 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratios $2:6$ and $10:30$ are equivalent. \n\nSimilarly, if we take the ratio $2:6$ and divide *both* numbers by $2,$ we get $1:3.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,2 &  & : & \\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,6 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,2 &  &  &  & \\,\\,↓÷\\,2 &  \\\\ \\,\\,1 &  & : &  & \\,\\,3 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratios  $2:6$ and  $1:3$ are also equivalent." :tutorial/id #uuid "cc2c9ce9-c2b0-5d68-81db-b8b0273407f0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8203 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6aa20fa4-3163-5025-84f7-a1ddd4324cd0" :step/math-academy-id 186681 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074635}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074635 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075035 :tutorial/content "Suppose that the following two ratios are equivalent:\n\n![](images/c6/c673bfcd099ef612f48bd0da7f8535cb9406d23088392e056884fb9415bb5100.svg)\n\nWhat is the missing number?\n\nTo find a ratio equivalent to $3 : 5,$ we multiply or divide both numbers by the same value. \n\nIn this case, we need to get $12$ from ${\\color{blue}{3}},$ so we multiply ${\\color{blue}{3}}$ by $4.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,3 &  & : & \\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,5 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓\\times \\,4 &  &  &  & \\,\\,↓\\times \\,4 &  \\\\ 12 &  & : &  & \\,\\boxed{\\text{[math]\\phantom{0}[/math]}} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to get ![](images/50/50f3c1b8dc3b1628130d1de11abcc455fc22006f32b7486f658c8134fcc3221c.svg) from ${\\color{red}{5}},$ we multiply ${\\color{red}{5}}$ by $4$ as well: \n\n$$\n{\\color{red}5} \\times 4 = \\bbox[3px,lightgray]{\\color{red}20}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $20.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3539d419-9d5a-5467-8a88-7ef318329859" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8204 :tutorial/title "Finding Missing Values in Equivalent Ratios"} :step/id #uuid "4854b84c-59a0-5a98-a9c6-9b8427de8ab8" :step/math-academy-id 186682 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074636}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074636 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073738 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073447 :question/id #uuid "a453e80d-016f-5a6c-9890-705463eb1fb9" :question/math-academy-id "e-13120"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3ac1e54-c949-5ffe-b377-b0e35240a13c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050924}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518125}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalent Ratios"} :step/id #uuid "55be2711-44d0-588a-899f-562ac0853236" :step/math-academy-id 186683 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074637}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074637 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075036 :tutorial/content "We can simplify ratios by dividing both numbers in the ratio by a common factor.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's simplify the following ratio: \n\n$$\n18:9\n$$\n\nThe numbers $18$ and $9$ share a common factor of $9,$ so we can divide both numbers by $9$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,18 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,9 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,9 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,9 &  \\\\ \\,\\,2 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,1 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $18:9$ is equivalent to $2:1.$ \n\nSince $2$ and $1$ have no common factors, $2:1$ is the simplest form of this ratio. We often prefer to work with ratios in their simplest form, where possible." :tutorial/id #uuid "c7ece136-e063-594d-b25d-4cf19bca7890" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8205 :tutorial/title "Expressing Ratios in Their Simplest Form"} :step/id #uuid "23872027-241e-5f61-8492-76236e405360" :step/math-academy-id 186686 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074638}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074638 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073739 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073448 :question/id #uuid "0a57949c-efc9-5403-9e7b-08346153f184" :question/math-academy-id "e-13121"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae17eb3f-f444-5ab3-82fd-6c92b97b0e41" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490320} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524017} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524993}] :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Ratios"} :step/id #uuid "092ffde3-3422-5950-9ca5-9b76fcb363ff" :step/math-academy-id 186687 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074639}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074639 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073740 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073449 :question/id #uuid "dd86c5a5-b8be-5d19-a477-dcf46683881f" :question/math-academy-id "e-13123"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d16202af-d3ac-54bc-9c76-25947a7a0b60" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515529} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515610} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521463}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Statement"} :step/id #uuid "36a366b2-6119-57ed-87f3-fd57834dc2d6" :step/math-academy-id 186690}] :activity/title "Equivalent Ratios" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073972}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490342} :activity/id #uuid "34509cbd-152a-5cd3-b3bc-231dc0557f02" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048508 :topic/id #uuid "a587db09-9578-5d61-b811-99a95c85061d" :topic/math-academy-id 553} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490342 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490312 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "311f524f-5dec-5e52-ac4c-9fb446367ae9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489626} {:answer/id #uuid "640e11a1-45f1-5bd9-ba70-d2bd9afff6a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489750} {:answer/id #uuid "6b8d1a24-f0b3-5957-bac1-4b551d6b4aff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489770} {:answer/id #uuid "736365bf-c5ec-516a-96d3-5dcf4aad4bc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489785} {:answer/id #uuid "8790ecbf-d319-56cd-88f6-19ef7c84fee4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489822}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489626} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7379773-3775-53b0-a3c8-4af0a853a08a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ddf5db22-7c12-5596-b4f6-5a61a8187c11" :question/math-academy-id "q-59828" :question/problem "If $10$ children ate $15$ cakes, what number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of eaten cakes to children is $3:\\boxed{}\\,$ because there are $3$ eaten cakes for every $\\boxed{}\\,$ children." :question/worked-solution "Since $10$ children can eat $15$ cakes, the ratio of cakes to children is $15:10.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $5$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,15 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,10 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,5 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,5 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,3 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThat means that $3:2$ is equivalent to $15:10.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of eaten cakes to children is $3:2\\,$ because there are $3$ cakes for every $2\\,$ children.\"\n\nThe missing number is $2.$"} :step/id #uuid "2b306b5b-d4d0-5bd9-ad85-47853cadb9b4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490388}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490347 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490320 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ab0f3fb-4e36-5cb7-9b92-bf6665005eea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2:1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489538} {:answer/id #uuid "4b95827d-b1f3-513f-a04b-4e3930a7a8ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2:2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489690} {:answer/id #uuid "9a2c18ff-74f1-5d6b-965e-9c4142e35106" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1:4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489857} {:answer/id #uuid "a3480f70-f3e4-5b52-9b49-7446ba7d2f81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1:2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489878} {:answer/id #uuid "c8575184-e89c-5e39-8b80-accc5525b223" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2:6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489949}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489878} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2e569b9-68b5-54d0-bd0f-496bb3345979" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490202}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ec0c1b9c-5490-5482-a264-c0fe31855e42" :question/math-academy-id "q-101444" :question/problem "![](images/ed/eda2132e8e050399298b97dc9d36cf27862d6aa4625e7aa870bb4c8fd246e781.png)\n\nWhat ratio describes the number of triangles to the number of pentagons?" :question/worked-solution "We count the number of triangles. We see that there are $2$ triangles.\n\nWe count the number of pentagons. We see that there are $4$ pentagons.\n\nSo the ratio of triangles to pentagons is $2:4.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers in a ratio by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,2 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,4 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,2 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,2 &  \\\\ \\,\\,1 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,2 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of triangles to pentagons is $1:2.$"} :step/id #uuid "338c847b-f4c7-5b56-ae4c-a5bc33c176b9"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490388 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490219 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b8636ec-a6ca-5436-8dd7-6da7ca001b95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489611} {:answer/id #uuid "b6f1e389-8ab5-503e-b205-cc5f0bcc7461" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489916} {:answer/id #uuid "c459ed53-041f-5cf1-a8ab-49b000eb425c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489940} {:answer/id #uuid "cd10f7eb-7f59-5705-9648-668ef168f8d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489970} {:answer/id #uuid "d3ee81e2-8914-5cd6-8aba-c7a5b8a811e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489984}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489970} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa08328e-cc55-5205-a1b2-f02f8b69f734" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490207}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1816745e-33d2-5605-bad0-d9ea4d6d5feb" :question/math-academy-id "q-59896" :question/problem "![](images/e6/e6c5263a440942da90d5ed6dc6a87e9e6caa0848dfb23c1a904eb828dfcaaeb1.png)\n\nWhat number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of rectangles to pentagons is $1:\\boxed{}\\,$ because there is $1$ rectangle for every $\\boxed{}$ pentagons." :question/worked-solution "First, we count the number of rectangles. We see that there are $2$ rectangles.\n\nThen, we count the number of pentagons. We see that there are $2$ pentagons.\n\nSo the ratio of rectangles to pentagons is $2:2.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,2 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,2 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,2 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,2 &  \\\\ \\,\\,1 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,1 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $1:1$ is equivalent to $2:2.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of rectangles to pentagons is $1:1$ because there is $1$ rectangle for every $1$ pentagon.\""} :step/id #uuid "f85a5132-bc8d-5028-bf7c-c2b4e0758ecd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490347}}] :activity/title "Equivalent Ratios" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489503}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515630} :activity/id #uuid "fbfe7b63-dd84-5bb9-ae0b-151a90820b22" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048508 :topic/id #uuid "a587db09-9578-5d61-b811-99a95c85061d" :topic/math-academy-id 553} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515630 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074373 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cf06951-cbcc-525c-9842-0890581834c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186072477} {:answer/id #uuid "b04ecb02-3d9b-5237-9855-274124892856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186072478} {:answer/id #uuid "d7352e89-2b19-5a8c-a0e2-f926cfe67ea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186072479} {:answer/id #uuid "1cf88531-0b03-5971-a795-d3362dd0c20c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17