below?\n\nThe ratio of students with blonde hair to students with black hair is $2:\\boxed{}\\,$ because there are $2$ students with blonde hair for every $\\boxed{}\\,$ students with black hair." :question/worked-solution "Since there are $8$ students with blonde hair and $12$ students with black hair, the ratio of students with blonde hair to students with black hair is $8:12.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,8 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,12 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,4 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,4 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,2 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,3 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThat means that $2:3$ is equivalent to $8:12.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of students with blonde hair to students with black hair is $2:3\\,$ because there are $2$ students with blonde hair for every $3\\,$ students with black hair.\"\n\nHence, the missing number is $3.$"} :step/id #uuid "27569124-56eb-5998-a405-7cf8748dd2b9"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518283 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518188 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45301129-1430-561c-975d-43df3220cccc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517623} {:answer/id #uuid "6c2a4ee8-1023-57d3-b19d-01bf628ad784" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517691} {:answer/id #uuid "a5e6ce96-e241-5d81-839a-77f0c738feb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517798} {:answer/id #uuid "a9f46437-76c7-59da-bffa-d2b285df56bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517805} {:answer/id #uuid "d4c4007a-d8a2-5dec-b9fd-1ec8181c9d5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517888}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517623} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0e97fe2-4795-50cd-a7b5-3d08d498d02e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c42ab941-f231-5a97-a430-88c5273648a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-59898" :question/problem "![](images/ec/ec8c3585db1981d001864b3c135f84e46643051d22f28e77f82e8b5a92d0f09d.png)\n\nWhat number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of squares to circles is $\\boxed{}:1$ because there are $\\boxed{}$ squares for every $1$ circle." :question/worked-solution "First, we count the number of squares. We see that there are $3$ squares.\n\nThen, we count the number of circles. We see that there are $3$ circles.\n\nSo the ratio of squares to circles is $3:3.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,3 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,3 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  \\\\ \\,\\,1 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,1 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $1:1$ is equivalent to $3:3.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of squares to circles is $1:1$ because there is $1$ square for every $1$ circle.\""} :step/id #uuid "b1a71eab-7b52-5146-8e98-812bf70a532c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518310}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518310 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518216 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4100d4ff-ed37-52a8-9450-a287631a77bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517616} {:answer/id #uuid "a6de607f-d322-5729-a80a-a71d1e3f5ccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517799} {:answer/id #uuid "be92aa79-3698-5510-9935-1b8212508906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517850} {:answer/id #uuid "d32b53af-b5dd-51b9-bb5d-778d1da7078c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517884} {:answer/id #uuid "d7ab97c6-7fa2-5c7d-b127-0ede64e61ee3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517897}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517799} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8f42b0d-1f1e-5535-971e-bc06dfe092ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518093}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f0e3ac9f-3533-517a-898b-d1cc891d8b27" :question/math-academy-id "q-59903" :question/problem "There are $15$ girls and $9$ boys in a geometry class. What number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of girls to boys is $5:\\boxed{}\\,$ because there are $5$ girls for every $\\boxed{}\\,$ boys." :question/worked-solution "Since there are $15$ girls and $9$ boys, the ratio of girls to boys is $15:9.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,15 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,5 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,3 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThat means that $5:3$ is equivalent to $15:9.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of girls to boys is $5:3\\,$ because there are $5$ girls for every $3\\,$ boys.\"\n\nHence, the missing number is $3.$"} :step/id #uuid "e6ad2332-1f0b-5002-894a-06b6c20121c3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518232}}] :activity/title "Equivalent Ratios" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517478}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180336} :activity/id #uuid "f75d5350-a041-5182-9b13-8e1a72d6079f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048639 :topic/id #uuid "b0e1b1ba-f451-5130-bf79-4959ebebab49" :topic/math-academy-id 3679} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180336 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180667 :tutorial/content "How can we subtract fractions, like $\\dfrac{2}{4} - \\dfrac{5}{4}?$\n\nSince both fractions have the same denominator ($4$), we can combine them into one fraction.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{4}-\\frac{5}{4} & =\\frac{2-5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we work out $2-5$ using our number line:\n\n![](images/c6/c63a3db44b79e751c7cf1a49dcebcbb1d00cea0427da4ef271d07646e4be2748.png)\n\nWe get $2-5=-3,$ and therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2-5}{4}=\\frac{-3}{4}=-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** If two fractions have different denominators, we need to put them over a common denominator *first* and *then* subtract!" :tutorial/id #uuid "a0129df2-078e-5236-8584-b81858a690fe" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3035 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4d9cc4ed-d42b-546d-ade4-504afd85f35a" :step/math-academy-id 16402 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180428}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180425 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179697 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179559 :question/id #uuid "bcf37a88-0ac3-59ca-8d23-8075f3eab872" :question/math-academy-id "e-9399"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd087cbc-c76f-5900-a1ef-183c383e34ed" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050637}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443837} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443841} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443842} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443843}] :knowledge-point/title "Subtracting a Positive Fraction From a Fraction: Unlike Denominators"} :step/id #uuid "a1dacde5-4449-5189-978d-83b7ce04030f" :step/math-academy-id 35180 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180426}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180426 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179698 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179560 :question/id #uuid "5b669372-7867-597f-a28f-806aa47fbb2b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9400"} :knowledge-poin