Positive Fraction From a Fraction"} :step/id #uuid "f428954d-d3e2-5c18-81f0-3d971ab4c9bb" :step/math-academy-id 35194 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180425}}] :activity/title "Subtracting a Positive Fraction From a Smaller Fraction" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179835}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079582} :activity/id #uuid "4d01100d-d331-5eab-a7b4-b31a3dd20fff" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049018 :topic/id #uuid "d44527c5-267a-5fa9-99b7-8ab45e08d4b9" :topic/math-academy-id 645} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079582 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079711 :tutorial/content "The **coefficient** of a term is the number part of a term, including its sign. For instance, the coefficient of $3x$ is $3$, and the coefficient of $-5x$ is $-5.$ \n\nEvery term has a coefficient, even if it has no numerical part. For example:\n\n- The coefficient of $y$ is $1$ because $y$ is equivalent to $1y.$\n\n- The coefficient of $-z$ is $-1$ because $-z$ is equivalent to $-1z.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0dd14810-5f7c-5799-b9f4-138d8de00b2e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8032 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "89085689-0df1-52dd-9e7f-3e0a74616260" :step/math-academy-id 71803 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079583}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079583 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078609 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078154 :question/id #uuid "16d88557-c94b-5666-a86b-ed241e6b106e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12819"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "33ebbaee-ad49-50c1-8973-c447756e0109" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050306}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078917} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079041} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444758} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444764} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444765} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444767}] :knowledge-point/title "Listing the Coefficients of an Expression"} :step/id #uuid "de9c0669-77d2-57ee-b99f-30a863da9b1e" :step/math-academy-id 71804 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079584}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079584 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078610 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078161 :question/id #uuid "c4668f0b-e26f-54d3-b1d2-e5ac6bb4efa5" :question/math-academy-id "e-12826"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64f7aa0b-533f-5141-bdec-5b053e7d8c6d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050637}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078715} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078916} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444761} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444762} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479074}] :knowledge-point/title "Listing Fractional Coefficients When the Variable is in a Numerator"} :step/id #uuid "0db80b9a-f601-5cec-9f17-f246ef2bf3ff" :step/math-academy-id 71808 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079585}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079585 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079713 :tutorial/content "The **constant terms** of a linear expression are terms that contain no variables.\n\nFor example, in the expression\n\n$$\n3x + {\\color{blue}{2}} -3y +{\\color{blue}{9}},\n$$\n\nthe constant terms are ${\\color{blue}{2}}$ and ${\\color{blue}{9}}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "275a2093-6068-5839-9c20-ef4db6931333" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8034 :tutorial/title "Constant Terms"} :step/id #uuid "e707b727-49f3-550b-b312-e84933e1d533" :step/math-academy-id 71812 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079586}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079586 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078611 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078157 :question/id #uuid "e731b5e2-f04a-50b8-95fc-f708d76a5343" :question/math-academy-id "e-12821"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b866c18-f3a0-599e-9bbb-970e0c79c585" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078902} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444759} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444768} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444769}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Constant Term of an Expression"} :step/id #uuid "e55cd1ca-93bc-53ba-a4f9-eb66f141140e" :step/math-academy-id 71813 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079587}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079587 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079712 :tutorial/content "*If a variable is missing from an expression, then its coefficient is zero.* For example, in the expression $a+2b,$ the coefficient of $z$ is $0$ because $z$ does not appear in the expression.\n\nTo understand why the coefficient of a missing variable is always zero, remember the following:\n\n1\\. Zero times any number (or variable) is zero. So $0z$ is equal to $0.$\n\n2\\. Adding zero to an expression does not change the expression. So $a+2b$ is equal to $a+2b+0.$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $a+2b$ as \n\n$$\na+2b+0,\n$$\n\n and we can replace $0$ with $0z$ to get: \n\n$$\na+2b+0z\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we see that the coefficient of $z$ is $0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ffa9a201-1851-51f6-b698-789b2379db46" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8033 :tutorial/title "Zero Coefficients"} :step/id #uuid "c67b45cb-0a77-5d83-9382-097fdca782c2" :step/math-academy-id 71817 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079588}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079588 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078612 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078156 :question/id #uuid "ab3ecbe1-5cfd-54e8-b380-6f76b28ae757" :question/math-academy-id "e-12820"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "444bd7e6-ade1-5ac7-8e53-d4b0735f93fb" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079130} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444760} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479140}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Missing Constant or the Coefficient of a Missing Variable"} :step/id #uuid "7e3d901b-ebef-5706-a523-9ff999c35440" :step/math-academy-id 71818}] :activity/title "Identifying Coefficients and Constants" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078667}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479148} :activity/id #uuid "b0b34d2e-4883-5737-85a2-37f53a7c5534" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049018 :topic/id #uuid "d44527c5-267a-5fa9-99b7-8ab45e08d4b9" :topic/math-academy-id 645} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479147 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479074 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2d5a746-cbe2-5c4e-8250-a72bbefe43f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478849}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478849} :answer-field/id #uuid "331590f2-60f5-540b-bbc5-f95dc66e46bb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478976}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2e46b72d-8394-58eb-a628-c7c43d6a0834" :question/math-academy-id "q-175333" :question/problem "The coefficient of $w$ in the expression  $\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{y}{2}+\\frac{4w}{7}$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The expression has three terms:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{3}\\,,\\,-\\frac{y}{2}\\,,\\,\\frac{4w}{7}\n$$\n\nThe only term with the variable $w$ is $\\frac{4w}{7},$ so $\\frac{4}{7}$ is the coefficient of $w.$"} :step/id #uuid "02e66efd-e8b4-5902-a4a9-8d1f150d690c"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479148 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078916 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0903e3e-77fb-5018-ad37-2dd943695068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{y}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076275} {:answer/id #uuid "b3c1f12b-65b3-5e6b-b7bc-56adb44622bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076276} {:answer/id #uuid "d9cdee4e-2c70-5cbe-a18d-a96b25e57c9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076277} {:answer/id #uuid "07d20f41-d333-53d7-b27e-a9610ba85080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3y}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076278} {:answer/id #uuid "78568bda-ae2a-53bc-bc27-2c7f33d28f3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076279}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186076277} :answer-field/id #uuid "c343a50f-a150-5152-98ab-d1462c81b537" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076274}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5fdf9d3f-882e-5327-869d-96a6a9c4c1cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-44237" :question/problem "What is the coefficient of $y$ in the expression $\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{3y}{4}+\\frac{11z}{8}?$" :question/worked-solution "The expression has three terms: \n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2},\\,-\\frac{3y}{4},\\,\\frac{11z}{8}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe only term with the variable $y$ is $-\\frac{3y}{4},$ so $-\\frac{3}{4}$ is the coefficient of $y.$"} :step/id #uuid "0c0a5827-c676-5c46-9702-6937639a0c10" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479159}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479159 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479140 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0697dad3-46f6-5d10-89fa-4c837a032b92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478569} {:answer/id #uuid "291f869a-c46d-58d4-8cdf-867a6f38c27d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478622} {:answer/id #uuid "5f26e716-5948-5421-bf61-bb04d45e2f23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478695} {:answer/id #uuid "5fe20a50-b7a3-59fd-86d7-e3fdf07429c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478697} {:answer/id #uuid "8ac1096e-c1ec-5138-80ca-f8a49701458c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478778}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478778} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec89f1b4-7246-58b6-b78e-5814d0d13e49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479051}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e9e375f3-04e5-5e92-84aa-677faf38ae91" :question/math-academy-id "q-67059" :question/problem "What is the coefficient of $a$ in the expression $6x-2y-7z?$" :question/worked-solution "We can rewrite the expression $6x-2y-7z$ as\n\n$$\n6x-2y-7z+0,\n$$\n\nand we can replace $0$ with $0a$ to get\n\n$$\n6x-2y-7z+0a.\n$$\n\nWe see that the coefficient of $a$ is $0.$"} :step/id #uuid "ffddaf4b-92f4-5adb-85c4-7ea2685659ca" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479147}}] :activity/title "Identifying Coefficients and Constants" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478560}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079416} :activity/id #uuid "24987256-107f-5a82-ab0f-aa09289e48f1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049264 :topic/id #uuid "e80811d9-e5d1-5c85-9386-86f6f8cb9ee3" :topic/math-academy-id 644} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079416 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079609 :tutorial/content "Terms in a linear expression are called **like terms** if their variable part is the same. For example, in the linear expression\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2x}+3y{\\color{blue}-5x},\n$$\n\nthe terms ${\\color{blue}2x}$ and ${\\color{blue}-5x}$ are like terms because both terms contain the same variable $x.$\n\nA linear expression may also contain multiple groups of like terms. For example, in the expression\n\n$$\n2a + \\dfrac{1}{2}b + 3a - 0.4 b - a,\n$$\n\nwe have two groups of like terms:\n\n- $2a,$ $3a,$ and $-a$ are like terms because they all contain the variable $a.$\n\n- $\\dfrac{1}{2}b$ and $-0.4b$ are like terms because they both contain the variable $b.$\n\n**Remember:** When a term has a subtraction symbol $(-)$ in front of it, the term is treated as a *negative*." :tutorial/id #uuid "dd5dd68d-3a84-5088-8ffe-a4f52618d31e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1352 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "deb2e31e-6ff8-541f-9e7c-df91c6033fb3" :step/math-academy-id 669614 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079522}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079418 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078506 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078370 :question/id #uuid "0dba136b-d72d-56a5-91bd-2f3cde60e600" :question/math-academy-id "e-799"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d06439e2-eba9-5cf0-be94-0beb379bc925" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049018}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079122} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079135} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444879} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444882}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Constants as Like Terms"} :step/id #uuid "78685eef-0c38-5b93-b9c9-d6fcdce7fb2b" :step/math-academy-id 669623} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079419 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078507 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078372 :question/id #uuid "b3e687e2-d4f9-5cbb-9b80-43d8904c3436" :question/math-academy-id "e-800"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e7bac34-87d8-5183-bdef-a68b66b3a212" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079134} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444881} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444883}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Like Terms when there are No Like Terms"} :step/id #uuid "28b1904c-01fc-5896-8cc6-c6e1339160bb" :step/math-academy-id 669619 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079524}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079522 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078572 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078207 :question/id #uuid "3e57a60d-9470-5d02-b5bb-880f9ee3de21" :question/math-academy-id "e-1538"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3abcb6ff-e659-520e-9bc3-ac19f88e8702" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050306}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079133} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444880}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Like Terms"} :step/id #uuid "cfc957cf-190f-528a-80f0-2faf92179217" :step/math-academy-id 669615 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079523}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079523 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079628 :tutorial/content "In some expressions, there are no like terms. For example, in the expression \n\n$$\na+2b\n$$\n\n there are two terms, $a$ and $2b$. These two terms do not have the same variable part, so they are not like terms." :tutorial/id #uuid "5bfd8a6b-a049-550a-848e-9c936685c24b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1699 :tutorial/title "Expressions with No Like Terms"} :step/id #uuid "4ec7c8ab-e45c-53e4-aab7-9f47bef74302" :step/math-academy-id 669618 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079419}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079524 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079629 :tutorial/content "*Constants are like terms, even though they do not contain variables.*\n\nTo understand why this is, remember that two terms are called like terms if they share the same variable part.\n\nAll constants have no variables, so technically they do share the same variable part. Their variable part consists of no variables at all!" :tutorial/id #uuid "09d3ea5a-55b8-51b8-8c8b-39642db6a8e6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1700 :tutorial/title "Constants are Like Terms"} :step/id #uuid "06c9ea06-b129-5e3c-8a12-fcef8fd2fb29" :step/math-academy-id 669622 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079418}}] :activity/title "Identifying Like Terms in Linear Expressions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078666}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148561} :activity/id #uuid "ce8303ff-2050-5cb3-8c68-076feb3951ea" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049467 :topic/id #uuid "f7d14b60-9964-50e8-9416-a68a8f8500eb" :topic/math-academy-id 2454} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148561 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148699 :tutorial/content "Percentages are *very* common in everyday life. For example, they are used in retail, finance, and sports! In this lesson, we will learn how to calculate the percentage of a whole number in real-world situations.\n\nFor example, suppose Daniel and Luisa traveled $200$ kilometers in their car. If Luisa drove $40 \\%$ of the way, how many kilometers did she drive?\n\nTo solve this problem, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{part} = \\textrm{percentage} \\times \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the whole is the number of kilometers traveled, which is $200$ kilometers. The percentage is $40 \\%,$ and the part is the distance Luisa drove.\n\nAs usual, we can use two methods to solve this problem:\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal: \n\n$$\n40 \\% = 40 \\div 100  = 0.4\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =40%\\times 200 \\\\  & =0.4\\times 200 \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo Luisa drove $80$ kilometers.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n40 \\% = \\dfrac{40}{100} = \\dfrac{40 \\div 20}{100 \\div 20} = \\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =40%\\times 200 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\times 200 \\\\  & =\\frac{400}{5} \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we find that Luisa drove $80$ kilometers." :tutorial/id #uuid "b32fe964-00bc-58b8-8e13-0ebde913bd87" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2080 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6f30bc9e-6f94-5aa2-a2a2-62ed9c078df2" :step/math-academy-id 7956 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148562}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148562 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147593 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147248 :question/id #uuid "f888277f-085b-5c6a-b50c-8ffd9095bc53" :question/math-academy-id "e-2327"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3626bdd2-ec13-5d74-a570-ec65ba682723" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050716}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147869} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442098} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442100} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442102} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442103}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Number Given a Whole and a Percentage"} :step/id #uuid "6d044eff-fad0-5933-9034-a4181e67deeb" :step/math-academy-id 7957 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148563}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148563 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147594 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147249 :question/id #uuid "03c003db-96d2-5588-a1fa-944b9305cf6f" :question/math-academy-id "e-2329"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "163d387b-3fd7-5f95-8245-5fb0184b371b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442095} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442096} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442099} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442104}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Number Given a Whole and a Decimal Percentage"} :step/id #uuid "61efc867-f64d-544b-ba1c-5cfc49f6333b" :step/math-academy-id 7959 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148564}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148564 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147595 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147250 :question/id #uuid "f409e34f-c8f7-5e21-b024-a0c7012c496d" :question/math-academy-id "e-2330"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "176069e4-4aa3-58cd-a04e-a70e7e5da960" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048806}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147872} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442101} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442105} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525012}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Number Given a Whole and a Percentage Greater Than 100%"} :step/id #uuid "55dcb5aa-7569-519c-a7c0-b114e3d83f67" :step/math-academy-id 7960}] :activity/title "Finding Part of a Number Given a Whole and a Percentage: Word Problems" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147655}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491237} :activity/id #uuid "9ea2bef4-3b98-5591-a696-0b49d8520af2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049467 :topic/id #uuid "f7d14b60-9964-50e8-9416-a68a8f8500eb" :topic/math-academy-id 2454} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491237 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147870 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b445a131-81f0-5514-a192-d5eb422bef26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$112$ kilograms" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144975} {:answer/id #uuid "c5e8430f-d1aa-5170-bb82-785391bf344f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$128$ kilograms" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144976} {:answer/id #uuid "d8a296a4-550e-588e-b32e-e9e4afbfb734" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$117$ kilograms" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144977} {:answer/id #uuid "f3b6ecbc-2867-5b7a-a528-e7f2ca3a3967" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$121$ kilograms" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144978} {:answer/id #uuid "7b0261e5-a932-5e3f-8956-c8d434bf15ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$130$ kilograms" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144979}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186144977} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0247569-e442-53e7-b321-6e1aaaeee8a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144974}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d7988730-acb0-5c20-b331-3dbab826b8f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-61174" :question/problem "A concrete mix weighs $600$ kilograms. Of this amount, $19.5%$ represents the weight of the crushed stone in the mix. How much crushed stone is there in the concrete mix?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the weight of the concrete mix, which is $600$ kilograms. The percentage is $19.5%,$ and the part is the weight of the crushed stone in the mix.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n19.5%=19.5÷100=0.195\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =19.5%\\times 600 \\\\  & =0.195\\times 600 \\\\  & =117\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $19.5%$ of $600$ is $117.$ Thus, there are $117$ kilograms of crushed stone in the concrete mix.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n19.5%=\\frac{19.5}{100}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =19.5%\\times 600 \\\\  & =\\frac{19.5}{100}\\times 600 \\\\  & =19.5\\times \\frac{600}{100} \\\\  & =19.5\\times 6 \\\\  & =117\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $19.5%$ of $600$ is $117.$ Thus, there are $117$ kilograms of crushed stone in the concrete mix."} :step/id #uuid "514173b4-add8-5a8b-884e-61c59887c63f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491267}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491241 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147872 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3fed532-6ecc-54f6-bad0-b6ad07df33aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$21$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144987} {:answer/id #uuid "2fbe64f4-a22c-5777-a3ec-19b32abc5173" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$22$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144988} {:answer/id #uuid "33f5d0f0-b1cd-5f13-9fb4-4005ac8072ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$24$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144989} {:answer/id #uuid "767643b6-d9d5-5d8b-94f6-d58fe32ca1f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$18$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144990} {:answer/id #uuid "7770f937-95a0-55a1-bb09-4a74b4daacc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$28$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144991}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186144989} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b14e3b7-0f48-5b1e-a3df-3a11bc0b2ed6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144986}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "290a6299-631d-5270-a788-c595be029595" :question/math-academy-id "q-61188" :question/problem "Anna, a dressmaker, bought $15$ meters of fabric in a particular month to make children's pants. The following month, she decided to buy $160%$ of the amount of fabric she bought the previous month. How much fabric did Anna buy on her second purchase?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the amount of fabric Anna bought the first time, which is $15$ meters. The percentage is $160%,$ and the part is the amount of fabric she bought on the second purchase.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n160%=160÷100=1.6\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =160%\\times 15 \\\\  & =1.6\\times 15 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $160%$ of $15$ is $24.$ So, Anna bought $24$ meters of fabric on the second purchase.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n160%=\\frac{160}{100}=\\frac{160÷20}{100÷20}=\\frac{8}{5}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =160%\\times 15 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}\\times 15 \\\\  & =8\\times \\frac{15}{5} \\\\  & =8\\times 3 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $160%$ of $15$ is $24.$ So, Anna bought $24$ meters of fabric on the second purchase."} :step/id #uuid "64ed2f2c-78f8-59b1-af54-b0439b650035"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491267 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491195 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29b3a452-1742-57c1-9f1e-bc77b9df5dd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "420" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490589} {:answer/id #uuid "61ce839e-834f-54a5-9c9e-f00fd0753c64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "360" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490693} {:answer/id #uuid "744129d7-d0ce-5c8c-98a9-76b25bb784a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "320" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490739} {:answer/id #uuid "b833ff6e-69ba-52a2-81d4-7ba41f29f665" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "430" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490876} {:answer/id #uuid "e1bd0ebf-e3a4-517c-90b3-10048e44189a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "380" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490938}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490693} :answer-field/id #uuid "f229c353-7ba4-5955-b6b1-8b5659543db4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c38eb0c5-c781-5447-a06b-aea59b9ebae6" :question/math-academy-id "q-61182" :question/problem "On the opening day of the children's baseball season, $300$ people attended the baseball game. The next game was attended by $120%$ of the number of people who attended the opening game. How many people attended the second game?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the total number of people who attended the opening game, which is $300$ people. The percentage is $120%,$ and the part is the number of people who attended the second game.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n120%=120÷100=1.2\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =120%\\times 300 \\\\  & =1.2\\times 300 \\\\  & =360\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $120%$ of $300$ is $360.$ So, $360$ people attended the second game.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n120%=\\frac{120}{100}=\\frac{120÷20}{100÷20}=\\frac{6}{5}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =120%\\times 300 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\times 300 \\\\  & =6\\times \\frac{300}{5} \\\\  & =6\\times 60 \\\\  & =360\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $120%$ of $300$ is $360.$ So, $360$ people attended the second game."} :step/id #uuid "c868de51-fc0a-5c14-a54e-be88e0c2566f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491241}}] :activity/title "Finding Part of a Number Given a Whole and a Percentage: Word Problems" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490506}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074901} :activity/id #uuid "71a6f31c-0bb8-5061-8779-9e3b7984d66e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049868 :topic/id #uuid "19c222a3-03e5-5eb1-b6c2-e026705f9eda" :topic/math-academy-id 573} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074898 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073898 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073587 :question/id #uuid "fb5d2269-5350-5d79-9414-1a25caa48d8c" :question/math-academy-id "e-2647"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d9b6948-92f4-58b3-8c06-fae61765dc57" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049688} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050517}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074466} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441867} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441869} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441871}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Horizontal Distance Between Two Points"} :step/id #uuid "123d2929-5563-5b7e-8588-e7734bb4eb42" :step/math-academy-id 8890 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074899}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074899 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073899 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073588 :question/id #uuid "1250785a-1ae3-5218-925a-0eac01281f3f" :question/math-academy-id "e-2648"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0708cd3-0608-5d57-ac14-4c16ef362473" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074465} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441864} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441872} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495173} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525009}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vertical Distance Between Two Points"} :step/id #uuid "f954535e-e2c6-5a01-85b8-f90fdccafc1b" :step/math-academy-id 8891 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074902}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074900 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073900 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073589 :question/id #uuid "e05457af-60c5-519f-935e-db465fc76efa" :question/math-academy-id "e-2649"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83bbdad9-c2a5-5caa-a4e9-2a0c904c605b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074468} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074469} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441865} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441868} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495116}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between Two Points: Crossing the Axis"} :step/id #uuid "3a335606-3bdb-5535-8956-9a3ababd7eb1" :step/math-academy-id 8892} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074901 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074978 :tutorial/content "The distance between two points in the coordinate plane is the number of units between them. Let's explore two methods for calculating the horizontal distance between two points with the points $(1,1)$ and $(5,1).$\n\n**Method 1** \n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFirst, we plot the given points in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/19/196c50295e8d8a95084c31226fb9319ede7b70603d894b6939e4615df583c29c.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $4$ units, so the distance equals $4.$\n\n![](images/4b/4b531e29616f3e8d7ea0c65761e59cfea224b9c0e756c68b7647c7271dc09493.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $y$-axis and then subtracting.\n\n![](images/6d/6d5c6760e15098dc381e639cd806b6410f2fa6d9049d11d5467427635c9872b6.png)\n\nThe point $({\\color{blue}{1}},1)$ is at a distance of $\\color{blue}{1}$ from the $y$-axis, and the point $({\\color{red}{5}},1)$ is at a distance of $\\color{red}{5}$ from the $y$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is ${\\color{red}{5}} - {\\color{blue}{1}} = 4.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "aeee8d0d-20f6-5700-8c35-c66e0eef8daa" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2199 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1961bdcc-cea7-5d78-88c1-4f69e1dab833" :step/math-academy-id 8895 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074898}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074902 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074979 :tutorial/content "When we want to find the distance between two points in different quadrants, method 1 works exactly the same, but method 2 is slightly different.\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $y$-axis for a horizontal distance, or $x$-axis for a vertical distance, and then *adding*. \n\nSo, what's the distance between $(-3,2)$ and $(1,2)?$\n\n**Method 1** \n\nWe plot the given points in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/e0/e0fec8fca43b421b62efc3d6d892563c80beaab3c2816be47aa7563868555268.png)\n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $4$ units, so the distance equals $4.$\n\n![](images/dd/ddc1e6cec323fe245ff2a115a587e773d408dc0115ba5002c02ac1d238620c63.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $y$-axis and then adding.\n\n![](images/0b/0bbb91fc8c0e7ad577261dcdbc8e7aaa16a44e8be2e937e2d6870aae58af4ccf.png)\n\nThe point $({\\color{blue}{1}},2)$ is at a distance of $\\color{blue}{1}$ from the $y$-axis, and the point $({\\color{red}{-3}},2)$ is at a distance of $\\color{red}{3}$ from the $y$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is ${\\color{red}{3}} + {\\color{blue}{1}} = 4.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a1233efd-87e1-5707-9b70-24c24ed47418" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2204 :tutorial/title "The Distance Between Two Points in Different Quadrants"} :step/id #uuid "d97c5e30-e125-5cba-9973-1e1898eca20e" :step/math-academy-id 8947 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074900}}] :activity/title "Distances Between Points in the Coordinate Plane" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073991}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495267} :activity/id #uuid "4ad6406f-28ca-5b57-94e6-4b4cc6fef04e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049868 :topic/id #uuid "19c222a3-03e5-5eb1-b6c2-e026705f9eda" :topic/math-academy-id 573} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495237 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495116 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bdc2701-e79a-5b33-86a6-ef27722a55d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494506} {:answer/id #uuid "6822bf50-caeb-554f-938f-a5cc8683af71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494526} {:answer/id #uuid "80300fbe-d90a-591a-928d-9196db5a2240" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494596} {:answer/id #uuid "b85c490c-92e9-5030-a0f6-291132b15fa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494748} {:answer/id #uuid "dceb000c-8083-5f83-9e97-ae002b6b6ba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494841}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494506} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a673c29-4fae-58d2-bcf6-86accdee64d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494972}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "95857c13-ed71-5f23-a1a7-f058676d3273" :question/math-academy-id "q-161670" :question/problem "What is the distance between the points $(-1,5)$  and $(3,5)?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1** \n\nWe plot the given points in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/74/74dfce8c8444a841f9f742d70204d82b22157bf5b955bb2691eb0cdefe62fb75.png)\n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $4$ units, so the distance equals $4.$\n\n![](images/4d/4df0d2515a43236634f934879d6a4d8926cc09163f79216ce288e07e2ba28e72.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $y$-axis and then adding.\n\n![](images/aa/aaf091301a2b004e80bef49a2893eed00f0a28693d8799ee366ed47425869a9c.png)\n\nThe point $(-1,5)$ is at a distance of $1$ from the $y$-axis, and the point $(3,5)$ is at a distance of $3$ from the $y$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is $1+3=4.$"} :step/id #uuid "a89caa7f-1651-5452-aad2-2238b199389e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495257 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495173 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42efa052-c9ab-54d8-9f6d-f34afac5ba39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494453} {:answer/id #uuid "6ec87af5-64e1-5fd2-bd75-166d861e724a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494553} {:answer/id #uuid "70a17d06-9360-5c19-9fa3-a5b4fb34974f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494559} {:answer/id #uuid "c731f4ef-fe9c-53df-889c-769685b74d4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494787} {:answer/id #uuid "ea8a321d-e3fc-594f-880d-2f2880f86d49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494873}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494553} :answer-field/id #uuid "7857e68d-f576-5073-be69-923b7ef4e7c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494970}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fce25e13-2247-5592-b858-9038960a704e" :question/math-academy-id "q-61243" :question/problem "What is the distance between the points $(-5,-2)$  and $(-5,-9)?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1** \n\nWe plot the given points in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/35/353bedad7a1692dea35f8b6adf30b7b461458c7852b4123a3fc865e1b23370a6.png)\n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $7$ units, so the distance equals $7.$\n\n![](images/e3/e3516729d7662a3b4438a3d3085da3428605d026ba3387a71afeb085e2bd3e73.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $x$-axis and then subtracting.\n\n![](images/f8/f8c5583d14d4e5ca50df3ce906254446c402177734f352169d794de2bb7dea5c.png)\n\nThe point $(-5,-2)$ is at a distance of $2$ from the $x$-axis, and the point $(-5,-9)$ is at a distance of $9$ from the $x$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is $9-2=7.$"} :step/id #uuid "d5842dc7-b1b5-522c-8151-6d2e56afbed8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495237}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495267 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495146 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4844ffa7-9148-54b8-9e72-b30c1cb0c11e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494463} {:answer/id #uuid "800b7dfe-1222-53bf-82a3-9afc3077f999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494595} {:answer/id #uuid "b32d799a-1971-505f-9d44-6d88dea78839" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494736} {:answer/id #uuid "cde81723-b01c-5413-aefe-9f6e7a2f5589" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494806} {:answer/id #uuid "ee3d1b5c-cd0f-5511-8f83-2c24cef83452" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494883}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494736} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce35d819-2898-51ad-bcff-556e53b6cc07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495016}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d345ec95-0d6e-5d8e-a681-8aa64a38a770" :question/math-academy-id "q-61238" :question/problem "What is the distance between the points $(-2,2)$  and $(-2,7)?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1** \n\nWe plot the given points in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/39/39ac0f7718d7ee4ca8ed96b43c4f5ff9b644e0a3f7b5e2629052468b36ccf687.png)\n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $5$ units, so the distance equals $5.$\n\n![](images/59/5995f7494a28a33cc4bdeef51d7d4cc0a85ee8e3219d8630d366c50345629d54.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $x$-axis and then subtracting.\n\n![](images/1c/1cb541f6fcbb93b30a8f154763b0daa60e3b76142db1001ce804a2a68ba79247.png)\n\nThe point $(-2,7)$ is at a distance of $7$ from the $x$-axis, and the point $(-2,2)$ is at a distance of $2$ from the $x$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is $7-2=5.$"} :step/id #uuid "ef3e2ccc-1312-5209-8ec4-fde9203f2c0d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495257}}] :activity/title "Distances Between Points in the Coordinate Plane" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494305}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111613} :activity/id #uuid "dc520173-74e8-57e7-ad6f-9e7a82ef78e1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049892 :topic/id #uuid "1c2006c0-c4c4-5ff8-8fa2-7f9dd3693f49" :topic/math-academy-id 1500} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111613 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111906 :tutorial/content "For any two distinct points, there is always one (and only one) line that passes through them.\n\nFor example, the line passing through the points $P$ and $Q$ is $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{PQ},$ while the line that passes through $A$ and $B$ is $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/8c/8c79a551e7ced71de157ea292baa3d7f38afaca4ea76ee467fc4add1b28ed33f.png)\n\nHowever, given three or more points, there is not always a line that passes through all of them.\n\nFor example, for the three points $F, G,$ and $H$ below, it is impossible to draw a single straight line that passes through all of them.\n\n![](images/30/30361c0bd49e571e317746f2d88848626da52e5ddbcda8fe9b6f496e4aff6b69.png)\n\nHowever, it is *sometimes* possible to draw a line through three (or more) points. In such cases, we say that the points are **collinear**.\n\nFor example, the points $M,$ $N,$ and $O$ shown below are collinear since they all lie on the same line.\n\n![](images/bf/bf3de55518c21de30718b2d9fee7032139c89aa3a4a963b8346b457ceafbeada.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "051ac95a-dc63-5270-a5fa-a498a36c8297" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6732 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2afa6da4-491a-5a31-8b50-09bd210fc929" :step/math-academy-id 40727 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111615}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111614 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110674 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110295 :question/id #uuid "a042ba66-3567-5b06-84e6-344ad0043bbc" :question/math-academy-id "e-10748"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f67c3319-1153-51ed-a57a-f85c06c7e76c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110905} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441210} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441212}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Points on Intersecting Lines Are Collinear"} :step/id #uuid "08fe2b06-8140-5116-be8c-f9a047f0e538" :step/math-academy-id 40728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111615 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110675 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110296 :question/id #uuid "76902823-634d-5525-afc5-5f2a98cea481" :question/math-academy-id "e-10749"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ef4e321-841e-5933-b5e4-ff7dab6c5275" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049350}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441205} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441211} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524990}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Collinear Points on a Grid"} :step/id #uuid "f4bbacca-99b9-57fe-b9c5-a2075ef9663b" :step/math-academy-id 40731 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111614}}] :activity/title "Collinear Points" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110859}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153002} :activity/id #uuid "26b78e45-1997-563c-b456-d53ae655f749" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049935 :topic/id #uuid "1fcf3c5f-af9e-5e5e-b7aa-ff9b3a312835" :topic/math-academy-id 2558} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153002 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153465 :tutorial/content "The **natural numbers** are the positive whole numbers:\n${\\color{blue}1},\\: {\\color{blue}2},\\: {\\color{blue}3},\\: {\\color{blue}4},\\: {\\color{blue}5},\\ldots$\nWe also refer to them as the **counting numbers**.\n\nThere's some debate among mathematicians about whether zero should be considered a natural number. However, for our purposes, zero is *not* a natural number." :tutorial/id #uuid "ddcec145-4774-553b-836d-76a4a5cd221e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9069 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0fa6983b-4c2c-5789-8e95-585a4e680b9e" :step/math-academy-id 172680 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153003}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153003 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152124 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151859 :question/id #uuid "960415f3-fe5d-53ea-a164-5960e800fb3b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14642"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0075774-6bd8-54c9-b698-4aacd499fce5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049429}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515579} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521562} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523984}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Numbers as Natural"} :step/id #uuid "bc685578-51b3-5ffb-b8b8-a5cca744297f" :step/math-academy-id 172681 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153004}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153004 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153466 :tutorial/content "When we extend the natural numbers by joining them with zero and the whole negative numbers, we get the **integers**.\n$\\overbrace{\\ldots, {\\color{red}-5},\\: {\\color{red}-4},\\: {\\color{red}-3},\\: {\\color{red}-2},\\: {\\color{red}-1},\\: {\\color{red}0}, \\: \\!\\underbrace{{\\color{blue}1},\\: {\\color{blue}2}, \\: {\\color{blue}3},\\: {\\color{blue}4},\\: {\\color{blue}5},\\ldots}_{\\large\\textrm{Natural numbers}}}^{\\large\\textrm{Integers}}$\n\nWe can represent the relationship between the natural numbers and integers using an inclusion diagram:\n\n![](images/00/00a2725bbe34eb8bc88f7f6dca6f22e624854a0cb469645eb2f4f9b79a38f846.png)\n\nNotice that the natural numbers are *contained within* the integers. In other words, *every* natural number is also an integer." :tutorial/id #uuid "4109da7f-f9b5-5c94-8215-06f5ea408038" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9070 :tutorial/title "The Integers"} :step/id #uuid "bfd8720b-23e8-55fe-8c02-ac787976007a" :step/math-academy-id 172684 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153005}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153005 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152125 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151860 :question/id #uuid "30b0a464-f903-5ee1-b649-97bfb366a73b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14643"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebf3f930-2768-5399-8470-48ac430065a3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433609} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433610} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524970}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Numbers as Integers"} :step/id #uuid "34d04639-2557-5679-9ab1-fa22b4733ce3" :step/math-academy-id 172685 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153006}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153006 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153467 :tutorial/content "The **rational numbers** consist of all numbers that can be written as the ratio of two integers. For example:\n\n$$\n{\\color{purple}-\\dfrac{1}{2}}, \\quad {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{1}{2}}, \\quad {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{1}{3}}, \\quad {\\color{purple}-\\dfrac{2}{3}}, \\quad {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{2}{3}}\n$$\n\nEvery integer is also a rational number because every integer can be expressed as a ratio of two integers. For example,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2} = {\\color{black}\\dfrac{2}{1}}, \\qquad {\\color{red}-9} = {\\color{black}\\dfrac{(-9)}{\\phantom{-}1}}\n$$\n\nLikewise, any mixed number is a rational number because it can be written as an improper fraction. For example,\n\n$$\n2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nis a rational number because\n\n$$\n2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac{2 \\times 3 + 1}{3} = \\dfrac{7}{3}\n$$\n\nWe can now add the rational numbers to our inclusion diagram.\n\n![](images/12/124ced6b317bdd2df4f967e6ea45e613b89d9fa136acb5edf8f7ed86fa8a4c06.png)\n\nNotice that the integers (and therefore the natural numbers) are contained within the rational numbers." :tutorial/id #uuid "3563ca37-8d82-5742-92b2-bb3918a71477" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9071 :tutorial/title "The Rational Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "cc715ab1-8b8b-555b-bd73-97d8d33107c0" :step/math-academy-id 172688 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153007}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153007 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152126 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151861 :question/id #uuid "471f680f-5a51-5dac-aa3c-834610690f0e" :question/math-academy-id "e-14644"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7063140-eebe-598c-b530-3c13509d359b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424829} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433607} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433608} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518173}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Numbers as Rational"} :step/id #uuid "ba79c281-b939-528f-8605-0a6cd2aa6abd" :step/math-academy-id 172689 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153008}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153008 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153468 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the following rational number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac13\n$$\n\nWe can find the decimal representation of this rational number by dividing $1$ by $3$ using long division, as follows:\n\n| $\\,\\,0 \\,. 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3$$\\:\\ldots$$3$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\,. \\,} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$1$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$1$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$1$$\\,\\ldots$ |\n| --- |\n\nNote that, in this case\n\n- the long division process never ends, and\n- the digit $3$ after the decimal point repeats *forever*.\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac13 = 0.333\\,333\\ldots\n$$\n\nThis is an example of a **repeating decimal**. A repeating decimal is a decimal where the digits after the decimal point continue forever but in a repeating pattern.\n\nIt's convenient to use the following special notation to indicate decimals that repeat indefinitely.\n\n$$\n0.\\overline{3} = 0.333\\,333\\ldots\n$$\n\nSome decimals have two or more digits that repeat indefinitely. For example:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.78^(―) &= 0.78787878… \\\\\n0.675^(―) &= 0.675675675…\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSome repeating decimals have non-repeating digits before the repeating part. For example:\n\n$$\n0.333\\overline{67} = 0.333\\,67\\,67\\,67\\,67\\ldots\n$$\n\nThe key takeaway for this section is that every repeating decimal is rational! In other words, every repeating decimal can be expressed as a ratio of two integers.\n\nFor example:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.3^(―) &= \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\n0.78^(―) &= \\frac{26}{33} \\\\\n0.33367^(―) &= \\frac{16517}{49500}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn a future lesson, we'll learn how to express a repeating decimal as a fraction. For now, you can verify these results using a calculator." :tutorial/id #uuid "28ff4afd-e8c7-511c-86a4-14d547e6c6d2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9072 :tutorial/title "Repeating Decimals"} :step/id #uuid "8cd64bc9-e39e-5f5c-9c0d-618b3294cff3" :step/math-academy-id 172692 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153009}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153009 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152127 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151862 :question/id #uuid "f0bf7717-e639-5889-92fe-7cdcce737a55" :question/math-academy-id "e-14645"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "be826645-6351-54ab-b57d-2c2a83a1b68d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050724}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152519} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152676}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rational Decimals"} :step/id #uuid "7c946365-2b4a-5987-84b7-84cce8c8a6f4" :step/math-academy-id 172693}] :activity/title "Natural Numbers, Integers, and Rational Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152314}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433611} :activity/id #uuid "92c46132-b219-52cf-940d-0d4674e4370a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049935 :topic/id #uuid "1fcf3c5f-af9e-5e5e-b7aa-ff9b3a312835" :topic/math-academy-id 2558} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433611 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433607 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "415659c8-ed57-5ad3-a27e-b7733ecf22bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453916} {:answer/id #uuid "92089b13-bdb4-5085-b38e-26d2ce7b7510" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453917}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453916} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4ca25b6-8beb-50ee-95d4-b2585267232c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454708} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dde4d56-adff-5ffe-b182-72bcc60b2763" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453918} {:answer/id #uuid "93299e16-4673-5a0a-bb3e-c8c38ed2ade4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453919}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453918} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b18629e-b61d-552c-b62e-a2d1e0b590a7" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454709} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5a5574c-eb8a-58f3-b104-88389929fd1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453920} {:answer/id #uuid "65e0b7ba-eb7d-5748-b3f5-a76e412f1f15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453921}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453921} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb1dcca6-d76c-5952-ab4a-38f43ed8d5a5" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454710}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "39fe4419-4a43-50fa-a07c-6ab5298bae4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-67819" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\frac{1}{10}$ is natural number\n\n- **II.** $\\frac{1}{10}$ is an integer\n\n- **III.** $\\frac{1}{10}$ is a rational number" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall our definitions.\n\n- The natural numbers consist of all positive whole numbers: $1,\\,2,\\,3,\\,4,\\,5,…$\n\n- The integers consist of all of the positive and negative whole numbers, and zero: $…,-3,\\,-2,\\,-1,\\,0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,…$\n\n- The rationals consist of all numbers that can be written as the ratio of two integers.\n\nNow, let's go through each number in turn.\n\n- $\\frac{1}{10}$ is not a natural number.\n\n- $\\frac{1}{10}$ is not an integer.\n\n- $\\frac{1}{10}$ is a rational number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "44dc1f4c-fd4e-52ad-a63a-2ecc1e1041de" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433613}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433612 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433610 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf08aba8-f0da-51fd-ad95-8655627dd121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475014} {:answer/id #uuid "7d0aa0db-a2a7-5129-a388-9c57ac3f39d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475015}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475015} :answer-field/id #uuid "227c914e-b720-542d-b308-cc175cdab060" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475433} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99c304dc-b34a-561c-b8ce-39248e0e4792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475016} {:answer/id #uuid "6c28208b-ec2d-5048-a2a3-e8269f75cacc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475017}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475017} :answer-field/id #uuid "b16204db-52e8-540f-a01c-bb228b5f48f6" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475434} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbd0222f-711f-5ac3-96b9-d66574a77070" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475018} {:answer/id #uuid "63cd09e1-6bb0-5cf1-8595-a1e09adda1ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475019}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475018} :answer-field/id #uuid "0073c129-f377-5688-a949-462c8471b3d9" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "eb2e9e07-c134-569e-a208-041621377fa3" :question/math-academy-id "q-99311" :question/problem "Which of the following numbers are integers?\n\nI. $100$\n\nII. $-5$\n\nIII. $-\\frac{9}{2}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The integers consist of all positive and negative whole numbers and zero.\n\n$$\n…,-3,\\,-2,\\,-1,\\,0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,…\n$$\n\n \n\nOf the given numbers, $100$ and $-5$ are integers, but $-\\frac{9}{2}$ is not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "63f2e9a0-ccd8-53e1-823a-f93d8c2355e1"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433613 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433608 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05b64885-2bbd-537a-8370-5a0fdd5a797e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454096} {:answer/id #uuid "c7d6ce83-198f-5020-ae74-adf1f4a11fe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454097}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454096} :answer-field/id #uuid "63e80334-99c3-51a1-a768-20f667e1870d" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454798} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e253825-73c3-528c-a9c3-17c1030fe9d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454098} {:answer/id #uuid "e4da0c49-de98-52dc-9c22-c42bfe19ff85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454099}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454099} :answer-field/id #uuid "320957d4-49dc-58ff-8a13-8882444c15f9" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454799} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f564e66-c448-5603-aa7b-b58b98664d6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454100} {:answer/id #uuid "8b4e5ec0-0acd-5415-bec0-f20a01d70ccc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454101}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454101} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc111e4f-9395-590b-8835-f911d5c47ee3" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "46c072ed-28fe-5e0e-96d0-a316d330f872" :question/math-academy-id "q-93611" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $-10$ is a natural number\n\n- **II.** $-10$ is an integer\n\n- **III.** $-10$ is a rational number" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall our definitions.\n\n- The natural numbers consist of all positive whole numbers: $1,\\,2,\\,3,\\,4,\\,5,…$\n\n- The integers consist of all of the positive and negative whole numbers, and zero: $…,-3,\\,-2,\\,-1,\\,0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,…$\n\n- The rationals consist of all numbers that can be written as the ratio of two integers.\n\nNow, let's go through each number in turn.\n\n- $-10$ is not a natural number.\n\n- $-10$ is an integer.\n\n- $-10$ is a rational number because it can be written as $\\frac{(-10)}{1}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "678e552d-7057-5896-a457-bfa4af875ece" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433615}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433614 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152878 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ded51ae-3828-55c2-9e45-b194a3a30cce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448858} {:answer/id #uuid "c3fc0999-add2-5a61-99f1-345783abd7de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448859}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448858} :answer-field/id #uuid "37f8f7af-8745-5efd-9267-e8a1ca839c2c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449469} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1492293f-4556-5254-ba65-c0b739554035" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448860} {:answer/id #uuid "53e198f5-a329-5d67-a0af-649a28e57496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448861}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448861} :answer-field/id #uuid "50641ce1-4436-54c1-85f7-3ccfc8587903" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449470} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37e674fb-807c-5cf5-aa23-ece394d4fe78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448862} {:answer/id #uuid "88cf372d-e313-5560-90f4-5ad9535887c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448863}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448863} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a0ae48d-fa38-5d85-b84b-0309468938dc" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c0d528fe-7741-52a6-a947-e2ff7f9a758d" :question/math-academy-id "q-98382" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $-2$ is a natural number\n\n- **II.** $-2$ is an integer\n\n- **III.** $-2$ is a rational number" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall our definitions.\n\n- The natural numbers consist of all positive whole numbers: $1,\\,2,\\,3,\\,4,\\,5,…$\n\n- The integers consist of all of the positive and negative whole numbers, and zero: $…,-3,\\,-2,\\,-1,\\,0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,…$\n\n- The rationals consist of any number that can be written as the ratio of two integers.\n\nNow, let's go through each number in turn.\n\n- $-2$ is not a natural number.\n\n- $-2$ is an integer.\n\n- $-2$ is a rational number because it can be written as $\\frac{(-2)}{1}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "9656a63a-73b0-53ca-9b94-ede50eec4768" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433612}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433615 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433609 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a7a4510-1d8a-5ae4-9117-2759f14527f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475008} {:answer/id #uuid "05e2564a-26ec-53b1-9105-eef524306174" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475009}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475008} :answer-field/id #uuid "887a1f56-eea6-509d-8bfd-0be97a597225" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475430} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3cdd3b1-f109-570f-aab0-c8a8042f40bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475010} {:answer/id #uuid "280a7746-17a8-5594-b498-4be355a8ebef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475011}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475011} :answer-field/id #uuid "86058e47-6f9f-51fd-a5ca-34c5d06d53ba" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475431} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "650b8815-0b38-51aa-9b3e-2e0ad39f65ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475012} {:answer/id #uuid "5dfbeb04-57b2-57b0-b551-d27531626ee2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475013}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475013} :answer-field/id #uuid "efdf8fd4-315b-58b9-97ad-de5cbc0003e8" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "de7db80c-f818-55cb-b186-c1d21190ea48" :question/math-academy-id "q-98369" :question/problem "Which of the following numbers are integers?\n\nI. $\\frac{3}{2}$\n\nII. $-3$\n\nIII. $5$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The integers consist of all positive and negative whole numbers and zero.\n\n$$\n…,-3,\\,-2,\\,-1,\\,0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,…\n$$\n\n \n\nOf the given numbers, $-3$ and $5$ are integers, but $\\frac{3}{2}$ is not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "c7a1ce99-411c-547f-b9eb-715734f5b768" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433614}}] :activity/title "Natural Numbers, Integers, and Rational Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433582}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065089} :activity/id #uuid "7d552a48-87b4-5e70-9852-ea30a169d751" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049948 :topic/id #uuid "208ad900-2568-5e64-a594-cff48ca19048" :topic/math-academy-id 299} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065089 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065361 :tutorial/content "Multiplying any number by zero *always* equals zero. Below are some examples.\n\n- $5 \\times 0 = 0$\n- $123\\times 0 = 0$\n- $-314\\,159 \\times 0 = 0$\n- $0\\times 0 = 0$\n\nTo see *why* multiplying any number by zero always equals zero, let's take a look at the multiplication table for $5$, shown below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5 \\cdot 4 &= 20 \\\\\n5 \\cdot 3 &= 15 \\\\\n5 \\cdot 2 &= 10 \\\\\n5 \\cdot 1 &= 5 \\\\\n5 \\cdot 0 &= ◻\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs we go down the list, the number in blue is reduced by $5$ each time. Following this pattern, we must have\n\n$$\n\\color{blue}\\square = 0\n$$\n\n.\n\nNote that, since multiplication is commutative, multiplying zero by any number (including itself) equals zero as well. Some examples are shown below.\n\n- $0 \\times 5 = 0$\n- $0 \\times 123 = 0$\n- $0 \\times -314\\,159 = 0$\n- $0 \\times 0 = 0$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7ed778da-8d5a-5363-972b-d9435e22a0e1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3119 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "77ea407f-55c3-5258-ba3f-15c16ab12b9a" :step/math-academy-id 16956 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065090}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065090 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064228 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064086 :question/id #uuid "078b5c64-d765-52da-9d87-56c23618c6a6" :question/math-academy-id "e-4635"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42032d61-c2d5-5154-9a9d-d93e69d1ba53" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064923}] :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Number by Zero"} :step/id #uuid "32f923a1-966b-5c2d-aa49-a238ba8d46c2" :step/math-academy-id 16957 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065091}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065091 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065363 :tutorial/content "Dividing zero by any number (other than zero) always equals zero. For example:\n\n- $0 \\div 2 = 0$\n- $0\\div (-456) = 0$\n\nTo understand why dividing zero by any number (other than zero) always equals zero, think about it this way: if you have zero pizzas, and you want to divide zero pizzas among ten friends, then each friend will get exactly zero pizza." :tutorial/id #uuid "65c0e3ec-7962-5911-a3c9-9de126e87edc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3120 :tutorial/title "Dividing Zero by Another Number"} :step/id #uuid "cb642d1f-f4bb-5b57-be91-7221332ef04b" :step/math-academy-id 16959 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065092}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065092 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064229 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064087 :question/id #uuid "897fedd3-db36-5614-886c-c88fba708692" :question/math-academy-id "e-4637"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2097574-c45c-504d-8ec8-44474880cd21" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064768} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515531} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525050}] :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Zero by a Number"} :step/id #uuid "28f3ce5f-977b-550b-9bda-7ae39c91c330" :step/math-academy-id 16960 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065093}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065093 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064230 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064088 :question/id #uuid "800d4367-20e6-55a9-8469-7c3654d512a0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4638"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3df16e28-70ed-5dd0-9bf0-d56b38f542eb" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064771} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521501} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524035}] :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Zero by a Fraction"} :step/id #uuid "a914b898-5054-5bf0-b58e-1526eea6a0fa" :step/math-academy-id 16961 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065094}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065094 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065364 :tutorial/content "Division by zero is **undefined.** No number can be divided by zero, not even zero itself.\n\nTo see why, notice that if we divide $6$ by $3$, we get