uestion/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ad8acc5-35d3-5b37-8f18-dee28d8f4bf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433056} {:answer/id #uuid "4a14b643-6602-5d82-a43d-fe4acf5c587c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433058} {:answer/id #uuid "94993fc9-ba89-5d7e-ba0b-44ffa85da2e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433066} {:answer/id #uuid "95e6ec2a-3bed-5c88-b4a1-9bcfd6f1f5dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433067} {:answer/id #uuid "c088db7c-17d3-5a62-bc77-81a67fffb752" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433071}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433071} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d0b58c9-0b33-5eae-9645-c413c3c2079d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433076}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "75341efd-b297-526b-aba4-58f021908c5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-75792" :question/problem "Compute the value of $0÷\\frac{1}{8}.$" :question/worked-solution "Zero divided by any number (other than zero) equals zero. Therefore, \n\n$$\n0÷\\frac{1}{8}=0.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "bb1fd126-7794-5985-b55c-e3b72348c559" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433088}}] :activity/title "Multiplying and Dividing With Zero" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433049}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172716} :activity/id #uuid "d1fb6020-a735-5706-80b8-bc04b0d8b879" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050318 :topic/id #uuid "3f7c8941-2bc1-5431-84b6-f34e556ea057" :topic/math-academy-id 3543} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172716 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173299 :tutorial/content "In a previous lesson, we studied some rules for determining if an integer is divisible by $2,5,10,4,$ and $8.$ In this lesson, we'll extend our understanding of divisibility rules to include $3,9,6,$ and $7.$\n\nWe have the following rule for determining if a number is divisible by $3{:}$\n\n*A number is divisible by $3$ if the sum of its digits is divisible by $3.$*\n\nFor example, let's determine if the following number is divisible by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n312\\,561\n$$\n\nIf we sum the digits, we get\n\n$$\n3+1+2+5+6+1 = 18.\n$$\n\nNow, since $18$ is divisible by $3,$ we conclude that $312\\,561$ is divisible by $3.$\n\nThe rule for determining if a number is divisible by $9$ is similar:\n\n*A number is divisible by $9$ if the sum of its digits is divisible by $9.$*\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "0b0f6b89-66c8-5e63-9825-60eb62f1271f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9390 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1794b655-2bac-5203-8fdb-02da477fb69d" :step/math-academy-id 172668 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172717}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172717 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171932 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171756 :question/id #uuid "b926f4d4-513a-5b05-8ce2-bedc39784ada" :question/math-academy-id "e-15012"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb8fa39c-a982-569a-b2e0-833c51259711" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048145} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048967}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172310} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172602} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432788} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432792} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432793} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432794} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515522} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523999}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Number Is Divisible by 3 or 9"} :step/id #uuid "b30e2ba3-941c-5d16-b861-02db07423b35" :step/math-academy-id 172669 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172718}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172718 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173304 :tutorial/content "We have the following rule for determining if a number is divisible by $6{:}$\n\n*A number is divisible by $6$ if it is divisible by both $2$ and $3.$*\n\nFor example, let's determine if the following number is divisible by $6{:}$\n\n$$\n353,856\n$$\n\nTo check whether this number is divisible by $6,$ we need to check that it's divisible by *both* $2$ *and* $3{:}$\n\n- Notice that the last digit of $353,85{\\color{blue}{6}}$ is ${\\color{blue}{6}}.$ Since ${\\color{blue}{6}}$ is even, $353,856$ is divisible by $2.\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- We now check for divisibility by $3.$ The sum of the digits is\n\n$$\n3+5+3+8+5+6 = 30.\n$$\n\nSince $30$ is divisible by $3,$ we have that $353,856$ is divisible by $3.\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\nSo, since $353,856$ is divisible by *both* $2$ *and* $3,$ we conclude that it is divisible by $6.$\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "955bbb9e-e7e0-552d-9ecf-ef5efd79239a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9441 :tutorial/title "Divisibility by 6"} :step/id #uuid "96c70f43-c735-5619-a8c7-49afb6e904cd" :step/math-academy-id 172672 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172719}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172719 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171933 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171757 :question/id #uuid "2d40d35a-54e1-5d01-9e0f-345a290336ef" :question/math-academy-id "e-15013"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b483f9f7-47a9-5725-b1f1-fe0ea39a7e96" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172311} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172603} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432785} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432791} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518187}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Number Is Divisible by 6"} :step/id #uuid "46310e8f-1f56-5e58-9836-71090df92551" :step/math-academy-id 172673 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172720}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172720 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173300 :tutorial/content "The rule for dividing by $7$ is the trickiest rule of the ones we've studied.\n\n*To check if a number is divisible by $7,$ we double the last digit, subtract the result from the remaining digits, and then check if the difference is divisible by $7.$*\n\nFor example, let's check if the following number is divisible by $7{:}$\n\n$$\n46{\\color{red}{2}}\n$$\n\nThe last digit is ${\\color{red}{2}}.$ If we double this digit, we get $2\\cdot {\\color{red}{2}} =  {\\color{blue}{4}}.$ Then, if we subtract ${\\color{blue}{4}}$ from the remaining digits, we get\n\n$$\n46 - {\\color{blue}{4}} = 42.\n$$\n\nNow, since $42$ is divisible by $7,$ we conclude that $462$ is divisible by $7.$\n\nLet's see some more examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "a5cdfa6e-09a2-5600-9083-a424aaf3d959" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9391 :tutorial/title "Divisibility by 7"} :step/id #uuid "8c02867b-43a4-5116-88da-bc7736bfb7a5" :step/math-academy-id 172676 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172721}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172721 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171934 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171758 :question/id #uuid "dadf214d-16d0-5dbb-aa89-89f4f8e7c79f" :question/math-academy-id "e-15014"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb0ae539-5348-5a2c-b226-c009ce6d6094" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172454} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172604} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432795} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432796} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521495}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Number Is Divisible by 7"} :step/id #uuid "777acec7-1e70-587a-80c0-746fd1cdb9e4" :step/math-academy-id 172677}] :activity/title "Divisibility of Integers by 3, 9, 6, and 7" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172213}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483449} :activity/id #uuid "12fb99b1-6cf8-5a19-b791-8128678b255e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050318 :topic/id #uuid "3f7c8941-2bc1-5431-84b6-f34e556ea057" :topic/math-academy-id 3543} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483449 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483402 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12afe4b3-21c1-5226-88fa-1a4798e15712" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482843} {:answer/id #uuid "2feba7be-006f-5377-a598-baf491efcc41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482897} {:answer/id #uuid "d4078e9e-cf26-5341-9d23-42f6201b2a76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483169} {:answer/id #uuid "f188ef76-ca50-506c-82c0-30d85353b6d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483216} {:answer/id #uuid "f9c0a5c0-452d-5300-bcce-eff988f09d98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483233}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483233} :answer-field/id #uuid "39f46cce-e058-5bf2-9268-00ee8313197f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bb148c29-2fa3-5a15-9be6-3a95df07f188" :question/math-academy-id "q-132399" :question/problem "Which digits, when inserted into the blank, make the number $35\\,832\\,4◻2$ divisible by $6?$\n\nI. $6$\n\nII. $7$\n\nIII. $9$" :question/worked-solution "A number is divisible by $6$ if it is divisible by both $2$ and $3.$ Remember that to determine whether a number is divisible by $3,$ we check whether the sum of the digits is divisible by $3.$\n\nFor the given number, the sum of the digits is\n\n$$\n3+5+8+3+2+4+◻+2=27+◻.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each of the given digits we can substitute into the blank.\n\n- If we substitute $6$ into the blank, we get $35\\,832\\,462,$ which is divisible by $2.$ Then the sum of the digits is $27+6=33,$ which is divisible by $3,$ so the number is also divisible by $3.$ Therefore, the number is divisible by $6.$\n\n- If we substitute $7$ into the blank, we get $35\\,832\\,472,$ which is divisible by $2.$ However, it is not divisible by $3$ because the sum of the digits is $27+7=34,$ which is not divisible by $3.$ So, the number is *not* divisible by $6$ either.\n\n- If we substitute $9$ into the blank, we get $35\\,832\\,492,$ which is divisible by $2.$ Then the sum of the digits is $27+9=36,$ which is divisible by $3,$ so the number is also divisible by $3.$ Therefore, the number is divisible by $6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "2d9e737d-3beb-558d-b409-89e4144e50cb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483467}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483467 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483410 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02938ac2-a904-589b-95cf-e1e584154abd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482814} {:answer/id #uuid "1e295efd-cfd7-5826-8bdb-23fabf9e7f01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482869} {:answer/id #uuid "291773ce-54e7-5de8-a209-c43955975d44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482884} {:answer/id #uuid "4185b1db-1e91-57f5-b0ed-f446a3cc2f83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482923} {:answer/id #uuid "65401151-5ed3-53b1-8618-d2d6f6d8473e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482989}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482869} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f364f54-37f9-5c16-b23e-1b97b8dd9c4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483283}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c4adaad6-9b04-5b58-a83e-194edc0437bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-132400" :question/problem "Which digits, when inserted into the blank, make the number $11\\,521\\,6◻6$ divisible by $6?$\n\nI. $3$\n\nII. $7$\n\nIII. $9$" :question/worked-solution "A number is divisible by $6$ if it is divisible by both $2$ and $3.$ Remember that to determine whether a number is divisible by $3,$ we check whether the sum of the digits is divisible by $3.$\n\nFor the given number, the sum of the digits is\n\n$$\n1+1+5+2+1+6+◻+6=22+◻.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each of the given digits we can substitute into the blank.\n\n- If we substitute $3$ into the blank, we get $11\\,521\\,636,$ which is divisible by $2.$ However, it is not divisible by $3$ because the sum of the digits is $22+3=25,$ which is not divisible by $3.$ So, the number is *not* divisible by $6$ either.\n\n- If we substitute $7$ into the blank, we get $11\\,521\\,676,$ which is divisible by $2.$ However, it is not divisible by $3$ because the sum of the digits is $22+7=29,$ which is not divisible by $3.$ So, the number is *not* divisible by $6$ either.\n\n- If we substitute $9$ into the blank, we get $11\\,521\\,696,$ which is divisible by $2.$ However, it is not divisible by $3$ because the sum of the digits is $22+9=31,$ which is not divisible by $3.$ So, the number is *not* divisible by $6$ either.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""} :step/id #uuid "6d652c62-b3a3-5b32-af56-3ba9c69395ee" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483503}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483503 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172311 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca7235df-ed54-56b4-a96a-f4dd62411d9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168730} {:answer/id #uuid "09345208-4c96-5ff8-aad9-4dc79f7055c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168731} {:answer/id #uuid "3f01a342-66a4-565d-8547-c05a295e8ace" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168732} {:answer/id #uuid "48110931-110b-5b28-8961-d5731a2dc0ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168733} {:answer/id #uuid "4b893675-cde8-51e7-879a-c1367e13459f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168734}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168734} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4005020-e950-5306-a22c-5bc62b40c40b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168729}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c81002d7-9f3e-57b8-8938-1545e4037d2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-132404" :question/problem "Which digits, when inserted into the blank, make the number $66\\,333\\,9◻0$ divisible by $6?$\n\nI. $0$\n\nII. $3$\n\nIII. $9$" :question/worked-solution "A number is divisible by $6$ if it is divisible by both $2$ and $3.$ Remember that to determine whether a number is divisible by $3,$ we check whether the sum of the digits is divisible by $3.$\n\nFor the given number, the sum of the digits is\n\n$$\n6+6+3+3+3+9+◻+0=30+◻.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each of the given digits we can substitute into the blank.\n\n- If we substitute $0$ into the blank, we get $66\\,333\\,900,$ which is divisible by $2.$ Then the sum of the digits is $30+0=30,$ which is divisible by $3,$ so the number is also divisible by $3.$ Therefore, the number is divisible by $6.$\n\n- If we substitute $3$ into the blank, we get $66\\,333\\,930,$ which is divisible by $2.$ Then the sum of the digits is $30+3=33,$ which is divisible by $3,$ so the number is also divisible by $3.$ Therefore, the number is divisible by $6.$\n\n- If we substitute $9$ into the blank, we get $66\\,333\\,990,$ which is divisible by $2.$ Then the sum of the digits is $30+9=39,$ which is divisible by $3,$ so the number is also divisible by $3.$ Therefore, the number is divisible by $6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} :step/id #uuid "e6219abf-feda-5f67-92ad-2d82ceb7152e"}] :activity/title "Divisibility of Integers by 3, 9, 6, and 7" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482798}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134590} :activity/id #uuid "de69022d-51f9-5731-9e6c-752a541e19de" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050428 :topic/id #uuid "484d0a1c-bca0-5a5c-8066-d983f4434a94" :topic/math-academy-id 2174} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134590 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135180 :tutorial/content "To **square** a number means to multiply that number by itself. \n\nFor example, to \"square $3$\", we need to multiply $3$ by $3.$ We write the \"square of $3$\" as $3^2,$ so we have\n\n$$\n3^2 = 3 \\cdot 3 = {\\color{blue}9}.\n$$\n\nWe would then say that \"the square of $3$ is $9$\". We could also say \"$3$ squared equals $9$\".\n\nThe number $\\color{blue}2$ in the expression $3^{\\color{blue}2}$ is called the **power** or **exponent**.\n\nThe act of squaring a number can be represented geometrically. For example, let's start by representing the number $3$ with the following collection of three items:\n\n![](images/4a/4a4dbb46401052829138bddc1653ae843babf4c5cfb6ba9fc40ca29ede99bd70.png)\n\nThen we can picture $3^2$ by creating a square with length and height both equal to $3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/cc/cc343eafb463e25ab429bafbc06216dad73037e5a02a2be9adfe046629636013.png)\n\nThere are $9$ items in total in our square, indicating that $3^2 = 9.$\n\nThe first few basic squares are shown below:\n\n| $1^2=1 \\cdot 1={\\color{blue}1}$ | $2^2=2 \\cdot 2={\\color{blue}4}$ | $3^2=3 \\cdot 3={\\color{blue}9}$ | $4^2=4 \\cdot 4={\\color{blue}16}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ![](images/ce/ce5db07809eb56891f31ac7fb0257edb42bd650f00c51c25857b02d1333165ad.png) | ![](images/61/6113e94a28fc50c7f1fe77ddf082d28d543aa9e2b6e65f82e8623a89f624f7db.png) | ![](images/cc/cc343eafb463e25ab429bafbc06216dad73037e5a02a2be9adfe046629636013.png) | ![](images/7d/7d56b21746fa8a0053fa32b6c2bc6d9393e13459cb65473e64589cc2f6a01ad3.png) |\n\nThe squares of our usual counting numbers are called **square numbers** or **perfect squares**. We can write them as follows:\n\n$$\n1^2, 2^2, 3^2, 4^2, 5^2, 6^2, \\ldots \\qquad \\text{or} \\qquad {\\color{blue}1}, {\\color{blue}4}, {\\color{blue}9}, {\\color{blue}16}, {\\color{blue}25}, {\\color{blue}36}, \\ldots\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "31cd70b9-a231-5c24-b6f4-1a3e714912b6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8102 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "532d5ecd-c046-546b-906b-aa64daf1f6f9" :step/math-academy-id 151107 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134591}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134591 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133734 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133507 :question/id #uuid "62c086b3-028f-528b-acd5-7bdc204dcb23" :question/math-academy-id "e-12960"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6f6a3fd-67df-5671-aa5d-08471b2ce1fb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048924}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443088}] :knowledge-point/title "Squaring Positive Whole Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "0708b802-2bb7-5823-be5b-b14cb353d3fe" :step/math-academy-id 151108 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134592}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134592 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133735 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133508 :question/id #uuid "4a393145-3e63-532b-a288-775fbc7acd15" :question/math-academy-id "e-12961"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eece577d-72b8-5a0d-89fd-1cfa63bf73fb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049253}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443084}] :knowledge-point/title "Squaring Fractions"} :step/id #uuid "70765661-2016-5e64-9ab9-d709f8ecd73b" :step/math-academy-id 151111 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134593}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134593 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135181 :tutorial/content "The squares of negative numbers are always positive because a negative times a negative is positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-)\\,\\cdot \\,(-)\\,=\\,(+)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSome squares of negative numbers are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{2}\\,=\\,(-1)\\cdot (-1) & \\,=\\,1 \\\\ (-2)^{2}\\,=\\,(-2)\\cdot (-2) & \\,=\\,4 \\\\ (-3)^{2}\\,=\\,(-3)\\cdot (-3) & \\,=\\,9 \\\\ (-4)^{2}\\,=\\,(-4)\\cdot (-4) & \\,=\\,16 \\\\ (-5)^{2}\\,=\\,(-5)\\cdot (-5) & \\,=\\,25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch Out!** When squaring negative numbers, the parentheses are important! \n\nConsider the following squared quantity:\n\n$$\n-3^2\n$$\n\nThis is *not* the same as $(-3)^2$. Instead, this is a shorthand way of writing\n\n$$\n(-1) \\cdot 3^2.\n$$\n\nAccording to the order of operations, we should square $3$ *first* and *then* multiply by $-1.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{3}^{2} & =(-1)\\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "38064eee-d6b6-5557-881a-ed03baadeed7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8103 :tutorial/title "Squaring Negative Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "4aa26ed8-8e70-561f-a525-88bd5896de45" :step/math-academy-id 151114 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134594}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134594 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133736 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133509 :question/id #uuid "55e8797b-62bd-5515-81a5-e1c13249c1cd" :question/math-academy-id "e-12962"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "46e8fb12-e59a-5aba-a9e8-2f872a89b915" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050287}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134323} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443090} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443091} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506593}] :knowledge-point/title "Squaring Negative Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "fa3f90d0-2a7b-5652-89e0-b78d798e323f" :step/math-academy-id 151115 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134595}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134595 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133737 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133510 :question/id #uuid "c07254e9-d6a6-55b1-bab7-4fe453c8cc69" :question/math-academy-id "e-12964"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c28f4ffc-5168-5e71-9d77-ae313dd462ad" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049194}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134322} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443086} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443087} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443089} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506514}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Square of a Decimal"} :step/id #uuid "bf5278f5-4ec6-5286-ae5f-3cbb68840333" :step/math-academy-id 151118}] :activity/title "Squaring Rational Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134034}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506603} :activity/id #uuid "26dc73c3-a573-5ce4-b269-7e050d805860" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050428 :topic/id #uuid "484d0a1c-bca0-5a5c-8066-d983f4434a94" :topic/math-academy-id 2174} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506603 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506514 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "552d15ae-c5c8-509e-8ca8-494431dec439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506055} {:answer/id #uuid "68e4b1c8-a132-5e30-a98b-556a99af5dac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506088} {:answer/id #uuid "996044d0-4829-58b0-acf3-4fda7d419060" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506179} {:answer/id #uuid "f4c01550-6c48-50cb-af06-6cd887f27626" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "27.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506360} {:answer/id #uuid "ffd4bfdb-d6a7-556d-9522-25b678ff5a2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506379}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506088} :answer-field/id #uuid "c64d54dd-1bd9-5b4c-bf85-d147344d6818" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "14d93e8f-2ff1-562e-a35c-f8de5c3818d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-40857" :question/problem "$(-5.5)^{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to calculate the product of $-5.5$ with itself:\n\n$$\n(-5.5)^{2}=(-5.5)\\cdot (-5.5)=(5.5)\\cdot (5.5)\n$$\n\nFirst, we ignore the decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{2} \\\\ \\boxed{2}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $55\\times 55=3\\,025.$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in the first factor ($5.5$) and $1$ decimal place in the second factor ($5.5$), so their product will have $1+1=2$ decimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $3\\,025$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n30\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,{{25}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${5.5}^{2}=30.25.$"} :step/id #uuid "044c8258-6998-590f-acbe-62a40481c0e9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506616}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506616 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506593 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52f8b7f2-814b-52b7-8406-e6c27493120e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506053} {:answer/id #uuid "586654e7-8734-5135-9af9-664cad8ca414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506061} {:answer/id #uuid "65eac8e3-d47a-5916-a461-305de8d9c4b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506084} {:answer/id #uuid "c7fa432d-1e24-5a86-b227-9bd8e7f5ffaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506265} {:answer/id #uuid "daa57dc6-1f16-5e29-96c2-37eb66dd98de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506303}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506061} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa969c6f-c49d-52e8-859b-a1950adae923" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506457}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "dffd7631-0175-532f-861a-5fc5f04bbe69" :question/math-academy-id "q-42826" :question/problem "$(-10)^{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "To work out $(-10)^{2},$ we multiply $-10$ by itself: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-10)^{2}=(-10)\\cdot (-10)=100\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2eb80d86-6678-5a3e-88d9-4f420dd31528" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506624}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506624 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134406 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e937730-041f-5478-a981-98a01c39c491" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "225" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132476} {:answer/id #uuid "b12bf7a3-2fd4-50b0-b302-33ed49888285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-225" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132477} {:answer/id #uuid "424db066-192b-5a60-b740-a16c46343edb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "115" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132478} {:answer/id #uuid "6eae60c3-f0ec-5c89-afab-06747c6d0dd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132479} {:answer/id #uuid "7f835a56-bce0-51c2-820e-eda856d85dac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132480}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186132476} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b96da10-424d-55ff-8df7-9396205c5033" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "30ca79b4-7e41-5f3a-9cf8-5de3bed64d2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-42831" :question/problem "$(-15)^{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "Since the product of two negative numbers gives a positive number, we have\n\n$$\n(-15)^{2}=(-15)\\cdot (-15)=15\\cdot 15\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate $15\\cdot 15:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{2}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n(-15)^{2}={15}^{2}=225.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "56ee66c4-5700-5c25-bdef-89a28b53c5bc"}] :activity/title "Squaring Rational Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505902}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074871} :activity/id #uuid "6d7f90a6-7b6b-52db-bbf3-10269a41623a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050481 :topic/id #uuid "4d30ea10-ea6d-511a-a7e5-cce5fffa2a64" :topic/math-academy-id 552} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074723 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073786 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073658 :question/id #uuid "b3b354ef-26fc-55dc-acdd-4b28c5f273ef" :question/math-academy-id "e-8139"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6a21dbe-0674-5fa2-b3ee-e4069115c6be" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050924}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434577} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434578} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434580} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434585} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515588} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518172}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Ratio as a Fraction"} :step/id #uuid "1c6ef1b8-edb3-5545-930a-73cc7c32d2b9" :step/math-academy-id 30450 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074872}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074871 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074966 :tutorial/content "We can write ratios as fractions by placing the first number over the second number.\n\nSo, what ratio describes the number of rectangles to triangles below?\n\n![](images/25/25426db008d4b246f36959f51433e0a5cebe609fc01f9539d4f45af353919261.png)\n\nFirst, we count the number of rectangles. We see that there are $\\color{blue}4$ rectangles.\n\nThen, we count the number of triangles. We see that there are $\\color{red}3$ triangles.\n\nSo the ratio of rectangles to triangles is ${\\color{blue}4}:{\\color{red}3}.$ As a fraction, this is $\\dfrac{\\color{blue}4}{\\color{red}3}.$\n\nWe interpret this number to mean that there are $\\dfrac{\\color{blue}4}{\\color{red}3}$ rectangles for each triangle." :tutorial/id #uuid "d0b88e38-94c5-52f0-8b5d-de261fe7f82e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2047 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "710f2d4c-d273-5b72-9ccc-f38b8242da5e" :step/math-academy-id 7777 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074723}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074872 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073879 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073558 :question/id #uuid "44e1e2da-6e2f-50b1-b982-dd5d4b5a432c" :question/math-academy-id "e-2265"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e191386-9391-5e3c-9f2f-f17d55f9b6ca" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048508} {:db/id #e